1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,024
The shelling had not stopped
for nine days.

2
00:00:03,593 --> 00:00:09,547
Fifty feet beneath what had once been the capital of
a continent, the concrete sweated

3
00:00:09,567 --> 00:00:15,451
and shook, and the dust came down from the ceiling
in slow grey threads, like sand in

4
00:00:15,471 --> 00:00:19,411
an hourglass that someone had turned
over one final time.

5
00:00:19,981 --> 00:00:26,032
At the end of a corridor, behind a steel door, a
man sat on a small sofa and listened

6
00:00:26,051 --> 00:00:27,890
to the city die above him.

7
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:34,170
He had started the war that killed tens of millions.
He had built the camps, and filled

8
00:00:34,189 --> 00:00:39,899
them. He had told a nation it was chosen, and then
he had marched it into fire. And now

9
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:42,204
the fire had come home to find him.

10
00:00:42,776 --> 00:00:48,731
Nobody who was in that room ever described his last
thought. We only know how it ended.

11
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:57,795
A single shot, muffled by concrete. And then, for
the man who fired it, nothing.

12
00:00:59,865 --> 00:01:05,337
History wrote its verdict in the ashes he left
behind. That should have been the end of

13
00:01:05,358 --> 00:01:05,968
the story.

14
00:01:07,739 --> 00:01:13,867
Somewhere else — in a world that does not exist, a
world with no Berlin and no bunker

15
00:01:13,887 --> 00:01:15,676
— a second man was dying.

16
00:01:16,245 --> 00:01:22,323
He was standing waist-deep in molten rock, in the
throat of a volcano, and he was still,

17
00:01:22,343 --> 00:01:23,569
impossibly, alive.

18
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:30,305
He had been a scientist and a soldier, a traitor and
a god in his own estimation. He had

19
00:01:30,325 --> 00:01:36,633
been engineered to be superior, and he had believed it
with his whole heart. He had wanted

20
00:01:36,652 --> 00:01:42,468
to remake the human race in a single breath of a
virus. His name was Albert Wesker.

21
00:01:43,037 --> 00:01:48,936
And above him, on the rim of the rock, two people he
had underestimated for the last time

22
00:01:48,956 --> 00:01:51,198
lifted a pair of rocket launchers.

23
00:01:54,567 --> 00:01:56,837
Fire. Light. Then nothing again.

24
00:01:58,908 --> 00:02:03,614
Two deaths. Two men who never met, in two worlds
that should never have touched.

25
00:02:04,186 --> 00:02:06,902
This is the story of what
happened when they did.

26
00:02:33,473 --> 00:02:35,949
The building had no name worth
remembering.

27
00:02:36,519 --> 00:02:42,449
Four storeys of tired brick on the wrong side of an
industrial park, on the south bank

28
00:02:42,468 --> 00:02:48,400
of the Christina River. A parking lot with more
weeds than cars. A security booth with

29
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:54,281
a space heater and a paperback. And above the glass
doors, in faded blue letters that

30
00:02:54,300 --> 00:03:00,439
had not been cleaned since the Obama administration,
a sign that read: Umbrella Research,

31
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:01,478
Division Seven.

32
00:03:02,048 --> 00:03:07,651
Nobody in the city had ever wondered what happened
inside. There was no reason to. The

33
00:03:07,670 --> 00:03:13,274
company that owned it was dying, and the people who
worked in it knew it, and the rain

34
00:03:13,294 --> 00:03:19,224
came down on the flat roof the way it had come down on
a thousand other forgotten buildings

35
00:03:19,245 --> 00:03:21,578
in a thousand other forgotten towns.

36
00:03:22,149 --> 00:03:28,020
On the third floor, in a corner office with a water
stain shaped like Florida on the ceiling

37
00:03:28,039 --> 00:03:30,709
tile, a man was asleep in a leather chair.

38
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:34,579
And then, between one second and the
next, he was not.

39
00:03:35,150 --> 00:03:41,132
He did not wake the way people wake. There was no
drift, no warmth, no slow return. He

40
00:03:41,151 --> 00:03:46,506
arrived — the way a gunshot arrives — whole,
and violent, and already moving.

41
00:03:47,074 --> 00:03:53,215
His elbow struck a ceramic mug on the desk. It
tipped, and fell, and he caught it an inch

42
00:03:53,235 --> 00:03:59,442
above the carpet, cold coffee still trembling inside
it, before he had consciously decided

43
00:03:59,462 --> 00:04:00,480
to move at all.

44
00:04:02,050 --> 00:04:04,728
He looked at his own hand,
holding the mug.

45
00:04:05,299 --> 00:04:11,718
It was a large hand. Pale. Steady. It was not the
hand he remembered. And it was, at the

46
00:04:11,739 --> 00:04:14,793
same time, exactly the hand he remembered.

47
00:04:16,163 --> 00:04:21,581
Two memories reached for the same body, like two men
grabbing for the same pistol in the

48
00:04:21,601 --> 00:04:21,891
dark.

49
00:04:22,461 --> 00:04:28,355
One remembered a bunker, a sofa, the smell of
cordite and concrete, and a nation that

50
00:04:28,375 --> 00:04:30,442
had followed him over a cliff.

51
00:04:31,012 --> 00:04:37,093
The other remembered a laboratory in the Arklay
Mountains, a mansion full of monsters,

52
00:04:37,112 --> 00:04:40,781
a virus that had rewritten his
blood, and a volcano.

53
00:04:41,350 --> 00:04:44,370
Neither memory was a dream. Both were his.

54
00:04:46,141 --> 00:04:52,064
He set the mug down, very precisely, in the exact
ring of dried coffee it had left on

55
00:04:52,084 --> 00:04:58,079
the desk. Then he stood, and the chair rolled back,
and he walked out of the office to

56
00:04:58,100 --> 00:04:59,060
find a mirror.

57
00:04:59,629 --> 00:05:04,158
The washroom light buzzed and flickered twice
before it decided to work.

58
00:05:04,728 --> 00:05:10,728
The man in the mirror was tall, and pale, and blond,
with his hair swept back from a hard,

59
00:05:10,749 --> 00:05:16,415
handsome face. Mid-thirties, perhaps, though the
eyes were older than that. He wore a

60
00:05:16,435 --> 00:05:22,569
black shirt, dark trousers, and the kind of calm that
belongs to a predator that has already

61
00:05:22,588 --> 00:05:23,639
eaten this week.

62
00:05:24,211 --> 00:05:30,379
He leaned closer. And the pupils, as he watched,
narrowed into vertical slits, and flared

63
00:05:30,399 --> 00:05:31,771
a dull, burning red.

64
00:05:32,461 --> 00:05:34,521
This is not my face.

65
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:36,079
No. It's mine.

66
00:05:36,598 --> 00:05:42,091
The voices did not come from his mouth. They came
from somewhere behind the eyes, and

67
00:05:42,110 --> 00:05:47,605
they spoke at the same time, and for a long moment
the man in the mirror said nothing

68
00:05:47,625 --> 00:05:51,108
at all while two histories argued
over the same skull.

69
00:05:51,798 --> 00:05:55,394
Where is this? What is this place?
What have you done?

70
00:05:55,913 --> 00:06:01,576
I haven't done anything. I died. So, I suspect, did
you. Congratulations. We appear to

71
00:06:01,596 --> 00:06:02,963
have been — combined.

72
00:06:03,483 --> 00:06:05,341
I do not accept that.

73
00:06:05,862 --> 00:06:07,692
The mirror doesn't care what you accept.

74
00:06:09,211 --> 00:06:14,824
He gripped the edge of the sink, experimentally, and
felt the porcelain creak under his

75
00:06:14,845 --> 00:06:17,286
fingers, and let go before it cracked.

76
00:06:17,855 --> 00:06:23,731
The body was not human. Not entirely. He could feel
the Prototype Virus in it, the way

77
00:06:23,752 --> 00:06:29,834
a pilot feels an engine through the seat — patient
and enormous and hungry, woven through

78
00:06:29,855 --> 00:06:35,733
every cell. Strength that could fold a car door.
Speed that would look, to an ordinary

79
00:06:35,752 --> 00:06:41,288
eye, like a man simply ceasing to be in one place
and beginning to be in another.

80
00:06:41,858 --> 00:06:47,271
The Wesker half of the mind knew all of this the way
you know your own name. The other

81
00:06:47,290 --> 00:06:53,209
half learned it in that moment, and felt something it
had not felt since nineteen thirty-nine.

82
00:06:54,379 --> 00:06:55,432
Appetite.

83
00:06:57,502 --> 00:06:59,221
That was when the light appeared.

84
00:06:59,790 --> 00:07:05,978
It was not a reflection. It hung in the air a foot
in front of the mirror: a thin pane

85
00:07:05,997 --> 00:07:12,257
of cold, white glass, edged in crimson, with letters
typing themselves across it one by

86
00:07:12,276 --> 00:07:12,545
one.

87
00:07:13,865 --> 00:07:17,548
Host confirmed. Integration complete.

88
00:07:20,105 --> 00:07:21,168
Secondary.

89
00:07:21,689 --> 00:07:23,990
Don't take it personally. It's a machine.

90
00:07:24,509 --> 00:07:27,127
A second panel slid out beneath the first.

91
00:07:28,447 --> 00:07:29,519
Status.

92
00:07:34,598 --> 00:07:39,454
He read it the way a general reads a map of
a battle he intends to win.

93
00:07:40,023 --> 00:07:46,047
He knew instinctively what the numbers meant. Ten
was an ordinary man. Twenty was the

94
00:07:46,067 --> 00:07:52,163
very edge of what a human body could do. Thirty and
above was something else entirely.

95
00:07:52,853 --> 00:07:55,329
Charisma twenty-four. That's
you, I assume.

96
00:07:55,848 --> 00:07:58,132
A nation followed that voice.

97
00:07:58,653 --> 00:08:00,701
Into a bunker. Yes. I remember.

98
00:08:01,853 --> 00:08:10,627
Warning. Viral stability, seventy-one percent. At
forty percent, cellular consumption

99
00:08:10,646 --> 00:08:11,351
begins.

100
00:08:12,942 --> 00:08:17,452
For the first time since he had woken, the man
in the mirror went very still.

101
00:08:18,023 --> 00:08:23,831
The Wesker half knew this problem. In his own world,
he had kept the virus leashed with

102
00:08:23,850 --> 00:08:29,725
an injected serum, measured and precise. There was no
serum here. There was no here, not

103
00:08:29,745 --> 00:08:34,282
yet. Just a building, and a clock, and a
number going the wrong way.

104
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:43,888
The Shop is locked. Points are earned through
conquest, loyalty, superior action, and

105
00:08:43,908 --> 00:08:46,038
the defeat of enemies.

106
00:08:47,628 --> 00:08:49,996
Conquest. Loyalty. Superior action.

107
00:08:50,517 --> 00:08:52,716
It is describing a state.

108
00:08:53,235 --> 00:08:56,821
It's describing a company. Same thing,
if you do it properly.

109
00:08:57,971 --> 00:09:07,865
Mission issued. The Director's Chair. Secure absolute
control of Umbrella Research, Division

110
00:09:07,884 --> 00:09:09,804
Seven. Seven days.

111
00:09:11,394 --> 00:09:16,962
He read the mission twice. Then he smiled, very
slightly, for the first time in his new

112
00:09:16,982 --> 00:09:17,284
life.

113
00:09:17,855 --> 00:09:23,576
It was not a nice smile. It was the smile of a man
who has been handed a pistol in a room

114
00:09:23,596 --> 00:09:26,288
full of people who don't know it's loaded.

115
00:09:28,356 --> 00:09:30,572
There was a knock at the washroom door.

116
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:33,456
Doctor Wesker? You in there?

117
00:09:33,975 --> 00:09:39,828
The voice belonged to a man in his sixties, gravel
over gravel, the voice of someone who

118
00:09:39,849 --> 00:09:44,302
had spent a great deal of his life telling
younger men to get down.

119
00:09:44,870 --> 00:09:50,879
Wesker opened the door. And the knowledge was simply
there, waiting for him, the way the

120
00:09:50,899 --> 00:09:56,769
stats had been: Walter Pruitt. Night security.
Sixty-four. Army, twenty-two years. Two

121
00:09:56,788 --> 00:10:02,796
tours he never talked about. A wife who had died in
the spring. He worked nights because

122
00:10:02,816 --> 00:10:04,370
he didn't sleep anyway.

123
00:10:05,062 --> 00:10:10,053
Heard something fall. Thought maybe you'd keeled over.
You been here since yesterday afternoon,

124
00:10:10,073 --> 00:10:10,793
you know that?

125
00:10:11,312 --> 00:10:12,465
I know that, Walter.

126
00:10:12,985 --> 00:10:18,783
He had not raised his voice. He had not done anything
at all. He simply stood in the doorway,

127
00:10:18,802 --> 00:10:24,163
a head taller than the guard, and looked at him —
and Walter Pruitt, who had once been

128
00:10:24,183 --> 00:10:29,732
shot at by professionals and had not flinched, took
half a step back without knowing why.

129
00:10:30,423 --> 00:10:33,649
Right. Well. You want coffee? Pot's
old, but it's hot.

130
00:10:34,168 --> 00:10:37,028
No. Thank you. Go home early
tonight. I'll lock up.

131
00:10:37,548 --> 00:10:40,086
Can't do that, Doc. Company rules.

132
00:10:40,605 --> 00:10:46,644
The company, Walter, is in administration in London,
and the people who wrote its rules

133
00:10:46,663 --> 00:10:52,631
are currently arguing over who gets the office
furniture. As of tonight, the rules are

134
00:10:52,652 --> 00:10:56,114
mine. Go home. Get some sleep. You
won't, but try.

135
00:10:57,634 --> 00:11:02,941
Walter looked at him for a long moment. There was
something in the old guard's face —

136
00:11:02,961 --> 00:11:08,269
not fear, exactly. Recognition. The expression of a
man who had served under officers

137
00:11:08,288 --> 00:11:13,659
his whole life and had learned to tell, instantly,
the difference between the ones who

138
00:11:13,679 --> 00:11:16,480
were playing at it and the ones
who were not.

139
00:11:17,171 --> 00:11:18,134
Yes, sir.

140
00:11:18,653 --> 00:11:21,426
He had not called anyone sir in
seven years.

141
00:11:21,995 --> 00:11:28,164
He went. And halfway down the corridor, alone with
the rain, he said quietly to nobody

142
00:11:28,184 --> 00:11:28,668
at all:

143
00:11:29,359 --> 00:11:30,705
Different animal.

144
00:11:32,725 --> 00:11:38,500
Back in the corner office, the man who was now Albert
Wesker sat down at a computer that

145
00:11:38,520 --> 00:11:40,807
was nine years old and ran like it.

146
00:11:41,375 --> 00:11:47,149
The Wesker half of the mind knew what to do with it.
The other half watched, fascinated,

147
00:11:47,169 --> 00:11:49,519
as the screen filled with the world.

148
00:11:50,090 --> 00:11:56,332
It took him less than an hour to understand where he
was. An Earth, but not Wesker's Earth.

149
00:11:56,352 --> 00:12:02,528
There was no Raccoon City here. No Umbrella
Corporation that had ever mattered. No mansion

150
00:12:02,548 --> 00:12:08,447
in the Arklay Mountains, no Redfields, no Spencer.
He searched his own name, and found

151
00:12:08,467 --> 00:12:13,472
a dentist in Ohio and a high-school wrestler
in Oregon, and nothing else.

152
00:12:14,162 --> 00:12:16,413
Nobody. Nobody has ever heard of me.

153
00:12:16,933 --> 00:12:20,211
Then the other half of the mind
typed a different name.

154
00:12:21,783 --> 00:12:23,525
He read for a long time.

155
00:12:24,096 --> 00:12:30,149
He read about the war he had started, and how it
ended. He read the numbers — the soldiers,

156
00:12:30,169 --> 00:12:35,825
the cities, the six million murdered in the camps,
and the millions more. He read the

157
00:12:35,845 --> 00:12:41,698
testimony, and the photographs, and the verdicts at
Nuremberg. He read that his name had

158
00:12:41,719 --> 00:12:45,772
become the single word the whole human
species used for evil.

159
00:12:46,341 --> 00:12:52,346
He read that he had lost. Not narrowly. Totally.
His country had been cut in half and

160
00:12:52,365 --> 00:12:58,937
rebuilt by the men he had despised, and it had become,
of all things, prosperous and peaceful

161
00:12:58,957 --> 00:12:59,576
and dull.

162
00:13:01,346 --> 00:13:04,302
If you were hoping he felt
remorse, he did not.

163
00:13:04,874 --> 00:13:11,365
What he felt, reading the verdict of history on a
cheap monitor in the rain, was not guilt.

164
00:13:11,384 --> 00:13:17,519
It was humiliation. The fury of a man who has been
told that he was not merely wicked,

165
00:13:17,538 --> 00:13:18,307
but beaten.

166
00:13:18,995 --> 00:13:20,703
They made me a word.

167
00:13:21,225 --> 00:13:25,336
They made you a warning. There's a
difference. A word can still be used.

168
00:13:25,855 --> 00:13:31,894
I will not be pitied by a laboratory rat who was
killed by two soldiers in a hole.

169
00:13:32,415 --> 00:13:38,010
Then let's be precise, shall we? We both lost. You
had a nation, and you lost it. I had

170
00:13:38,030 --> 00:13:43,496
a virus that could have remade the species, and I
lost that too. And do you know why?

171
00:13:43,517 --> 00:13:44,207
Both of us?

172
00:13:45,527 --> 00:13:51,480
The man at the desk leaned back in the chair, and the
monitor glow slid across his face,

173
00:13:51,500 --> 00:13:56,097
and for a moment both halves of him were
perfectly, terribly silent.

174
00:13:56,788 --> 00:13:58,663
You lost because they saw you coming.

175
00:14:00,062 --> 00:14:05,241
You told them. You wrote it in a book. You shouted
it into microphones. You put it on

176
00:14:05,261 --> 00:14:10,624
banners. You gave the whole world ten years' notice.
And the moment they understood what

177
00:14:10,644 --> 00:14:12,886
you were, they united to destroy you.

178
00:14:13,288 --> 00:14:19,017
And I did the same thing. I stood on a stage and
announced my plan to the only two people

179
00:14:19,037 --> 00:14:20,179
who could stop it.

180
00:14:20,700 --> 00:14:22,919
And what would you do instead?

181
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:24,649
Nothing they could see.

182
00:14:26,668 --> 00:14:27,917
The rain went on.

183
00:14:28,488 --> 00:14:33,197
Somewhere in the building a freezer compressor
kicked on, and hummed, and settled.

184
00:14:33,768 --> 00:14:40,014
And inside one skull, two of the most dangerous minds
that had ever existed — in any world

185
00:14:40,034 --> 00:14:43,080
— came, for the first time, to
an agreement.

186
00:14:45,149 --> 00:14:49,024
Dawn came up over the Christina River,
grey and then gold.

187
00:14:49,596 --> 00:14:55,836
The rain stopped. The industrial park woke up in the
ordinary way: a delivery truck, a

188
00:14:55,856 --> 00:15:02,244
gull, a man in a hi-vis vest yawning by a loading
dock. Nobody looked at the third-floor

189
00:15:02,264 --> 00:15:07,558
window of the brick building with the faded
sign. There was no reason to.

190
00:15:08,128 --> 00:15:13,857
Behind that window, a man in a long black coat
stood with his hands folded behind his

191
00:15:13,878 --> 00:15:16,023
back and watched the day arrive.

192
00:15:16,591 --> 00:15:22,751
He had seven days. He had no money, no army, no
name that anyone recognised. He had a

193
00:15:22,772 --> 00:15:29,077
company that was barely a company, a virus that was
slowly eating him, and a machine in

194
00:15:29,097 --> 00:15:33,586
the air that promised him the world in
exchange for taking it.

195
00:15:34,154 --> 00:15:38,562
He picked up the sunglasses from the desk, and
looked at them, and put them on.

196
00:15:39,250 --> 00:15:41,187
They didn't see you coming
either, you know.

197
00:15:41,706 --> 00:15:42,614
Who?

198
00:15:43,134 --> 00:15:46,880
The world. In nineteen thirty-three. They
thought you were a clown.

199
00:15:47,399 --> 00:15:48,467
And then?

200
00:15:48,989 --> 00:15:52,736
Behind the dark lenses, the eyes
flared red, and faded.

201
00:15:53,427 --> 00:15:54,431
And then they learned.

202
00:15:55,951 --> 00:15:58,615
The last of the night drained
out of the sky.

203
00:15:59,186 --> 00:16:04,984
And on the third floor of a building nobody had ever
wondered about, Umbrella's new director

204
00:16:19,675 --> 00:16:25,814
The staff of Umbrella Research, Division Seven,
arrived that Monday the way people arrive

205
00:16:25,834 --> 00:16:32,043
at a building they suspect is about to die — one at a
time, a little late, carrying coffee

206
00:16:32,062 --> 00:16:33,565
from somewhere better.

207
00:16:34,135 --> 00:16:40,337
First came Dana Whitfield, fifty-two, office manager,
payroll, human resources, procurement,

208
00:16:40,357 --> 00:16:46,290
and — because nobody else would do it — the woman who
called the plumber. She had worked

209
00:16:46,310 --> 00:16:52,039
at Division Seven for nineteen years. She had a
folder under her arm that she had not

210
00:16:52,059 --> 00:16:53,865
wanted to open all weekend.

211
00:16:54,436 --> 00:17:00,332
Then Doctor Howard Lessing, fifty-eight, senior
scientist, who had once been told by a

212
00:17:00,352 --> 00:17:06,250
very famous man at a very famous conference that he
would win a very famous prize, and

213
00:17:06,269 --> 00:17:10,585
had spent the thirty years since waiting
for the phone to ring.

214
00:17:11,153 --> 00:17:17,090
Doctor Evelyn Marsh, twenty-seven, postdoctoral
fellow, who had actually been there since

215
00:17:17,110 --> 00:17:23,114
six, because the equipment was free before the others
came in, and because she had nowhere

216
00:17:23,134 --> 00:17:25,456
else she particularly wanted to be.

217
00:17:26,027 --> 00:17:32,365
Tomás Reyes, twenty-four, lab technician, who kept
the machines alive with zip ties and

218
00:17:32,384 --> 00:17:38,358
prayer, and who ran a gaming stream on weekends
with four hundred loyal followers.

219
00:17:38,928 --> 00:17:45,913
And Doctor Nadia Okafor, forty-four, virologist, the
best scientist in the building, and

220
00:17:45,933 --> 00:17:49,734
the only one who had already
updated her résumé.

221
00:17:50,904 --> 00:17:54,328
They found the Director waiting for
them in the break room.

222
00:17:54,898 --> 00:18:00,746
He was standing at the head of the cheap laminate
table with his hands folded behind his

223
00:18:00,767 --> 00:18:06,548
back, in the same black clothes as the day before,
looking as though he had slept eight

224
00:18:06,567 --> 00:18:12,348
hours in a very expensive hotel. He had not slept at
all. He had read every file in the

225
00:18:12,368 --> 00:18:12,950
building.

226
00:18:13,639 --> 00:18:17,971
Sit down, please. All of you. Dana — you have
something to tell them. Tell them.

227
00:18:18,990 --> 00:18:20,186
I, um. Okay.

228
00:18:20,706 --> 00:18:24,418
Dana opened the folder. Her hands
were not quite steady.

229
00:18:25,109 --> 00:18:30,371
Umbrella Holdings went into administration in London
on Friday. The parent company. There's

230
00:18:30,391 --> 00:18:35,712
a firm — Harrow and Lisle — they're the administrators
now. They sent this over the weekend.

231
00:18:35,732 --> 00:18:40,821
They're going to sell off whatever's worth anything,
and close the rest. And the payroll

232
00:18:40,841 --> 00:18:43,897
account's been frozen. Friday's pay
won't go through.

233
00:18:44,418 --> 00:18:50,540
Nobody said anything. Tomás put his energy drink
down very gently, the way you put down

234
00:18:50,560 --> 00:18:53,719
something in a room where a
baby is sleeping.

235
00:18:54,407 --> 00:18:55,318
So we're fired.

236
00:18:55,839 --> 00:18:57,281
We're — nobody's said fired.

237
00:18:57,801 --> 00:19:03,601
Nobody needs to. Thirty years. Thirty years, and
they're going to sell the freezers to

238
00:19:03,621 --> 00:19:05,633
pay some solicitor in Mayfair.

239
00:19:06,653 --> 00:19:08,396
The Director let them talk.

240
00:19:08,966 --> 00:19:14,231
This was the first thing the staff of Division
Seven would learn about Albert Wesker,

241
00:19:14,251 --> 00:19:19,641
though they would not realise they had learned it
for weeks. He let people talk. He let

242
00:19:19,662 --> 00:19:24,927
them talk until they had told him everything they
were afraid of, and everything they

243
00:19:24,947 --> 00:19:27,228
wanted, and exactly how much of each.

244
00:19:27,798 --> 00:19:29,754
And while they talked, he read them.

245
00:19:30,326 --> 00:19:35,560
Dana Whitfield, who would do anything to protect
the people in this room, because she

246
00:19:35,580 --> 00:19:40,877
had never been able to protect her own son. Howard
Lessing, who did not care about the

247
00:19:40,897 --> 00:19:46,690
company at all, only about the credit he believed it
owed him. Tomás Reyes, who was frightened

248
00:19:46,710 --> 00:19:52,130
and hid it with jokes, and who would follow anyone
who told him confidently where to go.

249
00:19:52,150 --> 00:19:57,076
Nadia Okafor, who was not frightened, only
calculating the distance to the door.

250
00:19:57,646 --> 00:20:03,389
And Evelyn Marsh, who had not said a word, and was
not looking at the folder, or at the

251
00:20:03,409 --> 00:20:05,443
others. She was looking at him.

252
00:20:06,134 --> 00:20:11,990
Doctor Marsh. Your paper on vector persistence in
primate cell lines. The one that was

253
00:20:12,010 --> 00:20:15,750
rejected twice and published in a
journal nobody reads.

254
00:20:16,270 --> 00:20:17,521
Evelyn blinked.

255
00:20:18,212 --> 00:20:19,109
You — read that?

256
00:20:19,628 --> 00:20:24,557
Last night. Your third figure is wrong. The
conclusion isn't. You were right, and the

257
00:20:24,576 --> 00:20:27,758
reviewers were lazy, and you should
have fought harder.

258
00:20:28,279 --> 00:20:33,599
It was a small thing. It took eleven seconds. And for
the rest of her life, Evelyn Marsh

259
00:20:33,619 --> 00:20:38,335
would be able to tell you exactly where she had
been sitting when it happened.

260
00:20:39,824 --> 00:20:43,174
With respect, Director, that's lovely, but
it doesn't make payroll.

261
00:20:43,693 --> 00:20:47,404
No. It doesn't. Tell me about the
freezers, Doctor Okafor.

262
00:20:48,326 --> 00:20:50,306
The freezers were in the basement.

263
00:20:50,875 --> 00:20:56,876
There were fourteen of them, humming in two rows
under flickering tubes, and inside them

264
00:20:56,896 --> 00:21:03,034
was the only thing of value the dying company had
left: thirty years of cell lines, tissue

265
00:21:03,054 --> 00:21:09,261
samples, and viral isolates, collected by Umbrella's
British parent from places most people

266
00:21:09,281 --> 00:21:15,420
could not find on a map, and catalogued in handwriting
that only three living people could

267
00:21:15,440 --> 00:21:15,762
read.

268
00:21:16,452 --> 00:21:21,772
Most of it's junk. Some of it isn't. There are
isolates in here nobody else in the world

269
00:21:21,792 --> 00:21:27,233
has, because the places they came from don't exist
anymore. Verdant Axis has been sniffing

270
00:21:27,253 --> 00:21:32,391
around it for a year. And a contract research
company in Shenzhen sent us an email in

271
00:21:32,412 --> 00:21:34,880
August asking what we'd take for the lot.

272
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:36,420
Verdant Axis.

273
00:21:36,941 --> 00:21:42,808
The biotech across the river. Glass building, big
sign. Their CEO is a man called Graham

274
00:21:42,828 --> 00:21:45,418
Tolliver. He tried to hire Howard once.

275
00:21:45,938 --> 00:21:50,185
He tried to hire me for less than I make
here. Which is saying something.

276
00:21:50,703 --> 00:21:56,362
Across the river, visible from the basement's single
high window if you stood on a box,

277
00:21:56,382 --> 00:22:02,365
the Verdant Axis building glittered in the morning sun.
Glass and steel and green lettering,

278
00:22:02,384 --> 00:22:08,041
a lobby with a living wall, a stock ticker on the
NASDAQ, and a chief executive who had

279
00:22:08,061 --> 00:22:13,719
told a business magazine the previous spring that he
intended to be the next great name

280
00:22:13,739 --> 00:22:15,089
in American medicine.

281
00:22:15,659 --> 00:22:17,902
Wesker looked at it for a long time.

282
00:22:18,590 --> 00:22:25,020
A man who tells magazines what he intends is a man
who wants to be seen intending it.

283
00:22:25,541 --> 00:22:28,375
Yes. Which means he'll pay for the
appearance of winning.

284
00:22:29,893 --> 00:22:32,345
The rest of Monday was a telephone.

285
00:22:32,914 --> 00:22:39,009
The first call was to London, to a man named Peter
Fenwick, a partner at Harrow and Lisle,

286
00:22:39,029 --> 00:22:44,987
who had been appointed to liquidate what remained of
Umbrella Holdings, and who had been

287
00:22:45,007 --> 00:22:46,891
hoping very much to go home.

288
00:22:47,578 --> 00:22:51,891
Doctor Wesker, I'm afraid there isn't a great deal
to discuss. The Delaware division is

289
00:22:51,911 --> 00:22:56,520
not a going concern. We'll be selling the sample
archive as a separate lot. There's interest.

290
00:22:56,540 --> 00:22:59,208
The building lease will be
surrendered and the staff —

291
00:22:59,730 --> 00:23:03,799
The sample archive is worth nothing without
the people who can read it.

292
00:23:04,720 --> 00:23:05,587
I'm sorry?

293
00:23:06,108 --> 00:23:11,643
Twelve freezers. Four thousand boxes. The labels are
handwritten, in abbreviations that

294
00:23:11,663 --> 00:23:17,134
were never written down, by scientists who are
retired or dead. The three people alive

295
00:23:17,154 --> 00:23:22,944
who can read them work for me. On Friday, they won't.
And your buyer will own four thousand

296
00:23:22,964 --> 00:23:24,413
boxes of anonymous ice.

297
00:23:24,933 --> 00:23:30,877
It was not entirely true. It was true enough. And it
had the great virtue, in Fenwick's

298
00:23:30,897 --> 00:23:35,333
ears, of being a problem that would take
a long time to disprove.

299
00:23:36,022 --> 00:23:37,208
What are you proposing?

300
00:23:37,726 --> 00:23:43,687
A management buyout. The division, whole. The archive,
the lease, the equipment, the intellectual

301
00:23:43,708 --> 00:23:49,051
property, the trademark. Four hundred thousand
dollars. Closing Thursday. You get to go

302
00:23:49,071 --> 00:23:54,846
home, Mister Fenwick, and write in your report that you
recovered value from a dead subsidiary

303
00:23:54,866 --> 00:23:55,647
in five days.

304
00:23:56,170 --> 00:23:58,304
And you have four hundred
thousand dollars?

305
00:23:58,824 --> 00:24:00,019
I will on Thursday.

306
00:24:01,539 --> 00:24:07,353
There was a silence on the line in which a tired man
in London weighed the effort of saying

307
00:24:07,373 --> 00:24:09,789
no against the pleasure of saying yes.

308
00:24:10,480 --> 00:24:12,100
Send me something in writing.

309
00:24:13,420 --> 00:24:16,397
The second thing he did was
create a company.

310
00:24:16,970 --> 00:24:22,862
In the State of Delaware, it is possible to bring a
limited liability company into legal

311
00:24:22,882 --> 00:24:28,641
existence faster than a pizza can be delivered.
Dana, who had filed paperwork with the

312
00:24:28,661 --> 00:24:34,485
state for nineteen years, sat beside him and watched
him do it in fourteen minutes, and

313
00:24:34,505 --> 00:24:38,518
felt a small, strange thrill she had
not felt since college.

314
00:24:39,208 --> 00:24:40,313
What are you calling it?

315
00:24:40,833 --> 00:24:41,624
Umbrella.

316
00:24:42,143 --> 00:24:43,231
Just — Umbrella?

317
00:24:43,751 --> 00:24:46,931
Every great name starts out sounding
like an accessory.

318
00:24:48,251 --> 00:24:50,667
The third call was across the river.

319
00:24:51,237 --> 00:24:57,487
Graham Tolliver took it himself, which told Wesker
most of what he needed to know. A chief

320
00:24:57,507 --> 00:25:03,757
executive who answers his own phone to an unknown
number is a man who is afraid of missing

321
00:25:03,777 --> 00:25:04,452
something.

322
00:25:05,144 --> 00:25:09,473
Doctor Wesker! We haven't met, but I know Division
Seven. Terrible news about London.

323
00:25:09,493 --> 00:25:12,953
Genuinely. If there's anything Verdant Axis
can do for your people —

324
00:25:13,473 --> 00:25:15,067
There is. You want the archive.

325
00:25:15,987 --> 00:25:19,891
I — well. We'd certainly be interested in a
conversation about the archive.

326
00:25:20,413 --> 00:25:26,356
Then let's not have a conversation. Let's have an
agreement. As of Thursday, I own it.

327
00:25:26,376 --> 00:25:32,735
You can license access to it for one point two million
dollars. Paid into escrow on signing,

328
00:25:32,755 --> 00:25:34,330
released on my closing.

329
00:25:34,848 --> 00:25:37,152
That's a lot of money for a
freezer, Doctor.

330
00:25:37,673 --> 00:25:39,717
The people in Shenzhen didn't think so.

331
00:25:41,235 --> 00:25:46,880
He did not say that the people in Shenzhen had made
an offer. He did not say they had

332
00:25:46,900 --> 00:25:52,545
made anything at all. He simply let the word sit on
the line, like a coin on a table,

333
00:25:52,565 --> 00:25:57,010
and let Graham Tolliver imagine what was
written on the other side.

334
00:25:57,579 --> 00:26:03,704
Men like Tolliver always imagine the worst. It is
what makes them rich. It is also what

335
00:26:03,724 --> 00:26:04,833
makes them easy.

336
00:26:05,525 --> 00:26:06,875
Send me the term sheet.

337
00:26:08,394 --> 00:26:10,713
The term sheet was four pages long.

338
00:26:11,284 --> 00:26:17,135
On page three, in the eleventh clause, in language
that any competent lawyer would have

339
00:26:17,155 --> 00:26:23,007
understood and that Verdant Axis's competent lawyers
would read the following week, was

340
00:26:23,027 --> 00:26:24,964
a single word: non-exclusive.

341
00:26:25,534 --> 00:26:31,938
Verdant Axis was not buying the archive. It was
buying the right to use it — alongside

342
00:26:31,958 --> 00:26:38,362
anyone else Umbrella chose to sell the same right
to. As many times as Umbrella liked.

343
00:26:39,052 --> 00:26:40,589
He will discover it.

344
00:26:41,108 --> 00:26:46,449
Of course he will. After the money has cleared. And
then he'll have a choice — to admit

345
00:26:46,469 --> 00:26:51,748
to his board that he didn't read a four-page
contract, or to keep quiet and pretend he

346
00:26:51,768 --> 00:26:55,382
meant to do it. Which do you think a
man like that chooses?

347
00:26:55,902 --> 00:26:59,592
You have become very
comfortable with lying.

348
00:27:00,112 --> 00:27:02,202
You were a head of state. Don't
be precious.

349
00:27:03,923 --> 00:27:05,931
The money moved on Thursday night.

350
00:27:06,500 --> 00:27:12,543
It moved the way money moves when nobody is quite sure
it should: slowly at first, through

351
00:27:12,563 --> 00:27:18,471
escrow accounts and compliance holds and a nervous
banker in Wilmington who called twice

352
00:27:18,491 --> 00:27:22,717
to ask whether Doctor Wesker was
certain, and then all at once.

353
00:27:23,286 --> 00:27:29,242
One point two million dollars from Verdant Axis
Therapeutics, into escrow. Four hundred

354
00:27:29,262 --> 00:27:35,424
thousand from escrow to Harrow and Lisle, in London,
where Peter Fenwick signed a transfer

355
00:27:35,444 --> 00:27:41,744
of assets at five in the morning his time and went back
to bed. And the rest — eight hundred

356
00:27:41,764 --> 00:27:47,240
thousand dollars — into the account of a company
that had not existed on Monday.

357
00:27:47,810 --> 00:27:53,527
At nine fifty-eight, Dana Whitfield pressed a
button, and the payroll of Division Seven

358
00:27:53,548 --> 00:27:54,648
went out on time.

359
00:27:56,217 --> 00:28:01,936
She did not cry. She wanted that noted, later, when
she told the story. She went out to

360
00:28:01,956 --> 00:28:07,675
the parking lot and sat in her car for a while with
the engine off, and she did not cry

361
00:28:07,695 --> 00:28:08,137
at all.

362
00:28:09,906 --> 00:28:14,625
Upstairs, in the corner office, the air in
front of Albert Wesker lit up.

363
00:28:15,944 --> 00:28:19,045
Mission complete. The Director's Chair.

364
00:28:24,480 --> 00:28:27,505
Acquisition. The name is yours.

365
00:28:29,094 --> 00:28:31,045
He read the second panel twice.

366
00:28:31,616 --> 00:28:37,719
The trademark. Harrow and Lisle had thrown it into
the deal as an afterthought — a dead

367
00:28:37,739 --> 00:28:43,699
brand of a dead company, worth, by their valuation,
one British pound. It was on page

368
00:28:43,719 --> 00:28:48,556
nine of the transfer, under miscellaneous.
Nobody had asked about it.

369
00:28:49,125 --> 00:28:55,697
The name was his. In every country on Earth that
recognised a trademark, the word Umbrella,

370
00:28:55,717 --> 00:29:02,000
attached to medicine, now belonged to a man who had
been alive, in this world, for four

371
00:29:02,020 --> 00:29:02,364
days.

372
00:29:03,683 --> 00:29:05,668
Level up. Level two.

373
00:29:07,987 --> 00:29:13,911
He placed the five points without hesitating. Three
into Intelligence. Two into Health,

374
00:29:13,931 --> 00:29:17,601
against the clock that was still
running in his blood.

375
00:29:18,920 --> 00:29:22,505
Viral stability, sixty-six percent.

376
00:29:24,096 --> 00:29:25,731
Five days. Five percent.

377
00:29:26,251 --> 00:29:30,199
And the machine gave you money for a
trick with a telephone.

378
00:29:30,721 --> 00:29:34,210
The machine gave us money for winning.
It doesn't care how.

379
00:29:35,728 --> 00:29:41,074
On Friday morning, the staff of Division Seven
gathered in the break room again.

380
00:29:41,644 --> 00:29:47,655
It was Dana who told them. That was his decision. In
the car on Thursday night, the voice

381
00:29:47,675 --> 00:29:51,654
at the back of his mind had wanted
something else entirely.

382
00:29:52,346 --> 00:29:57,837
They should hear it from you. They should know whose
hand saved them. A people must know

383
00:29:57,857 --> 00:29:58,589
its saviour.

384
00:29:59,108 --> 00:30:02,561
They're not a people. They're six
employees and a coffee machine.

385
00:30:03,083 --> 00:30:05,795
Every people begins as a room.

386
00:30:06,313 --> 00:30:12,115
Then let the room hear it from someone it trusts.
Credit is currency. I'll spend it where

387
00:30:12,134 --> 00:30:13,226
it buys the most.

388
00:30:14,347 --> 00:30:19,103
So — the short version is, we're not closing. The
Director bought the division. All of

389
00:30:19,123 --> 00:30:24,210
it. The freezers, the building, the name. We have a new
company, and it's — it's us. Payroll

390
00:30:24,230 --> 00:30:26,487
went out last night. Check your accounts.

391
00:30:27,007 --> 00:30:32,711
Five phones came out of five pockets. For a moment
there was only the sound of people

392
00:30:32,731 --> 00:30:33,855
reading a number.

393
00:30:34,545 --> 00:30:36,056
Oh my God. It's real.

394
00:30:36,576 --> 00:30:42,809
Tomás started clapping. After a moment, so did the
others. Even Lessing, who clapped the

395
00:30:42,829 --> 00:30:46,221
way a man signs a document he has
not agreed to.

396
00:30:46,789 --> 00:30:52,400
Only Nadia Okafor did not clap. She looked at the
man in the doorway, and she thought

397
00:30:52,420 --> 00:30:58,228
about the phrase Dana had used — the Director bought
the division — and she wondered how

398
00:30:58,248 --> 00:31:03,858
a research director with a four-year-old car and no
savings she had ever heard of had

399
00:31:03,878 --> 00:31:05,382
bought anything at all.

400
00:31:05,952 --> 00:31:11,681
He met her eyes across the room. And smiled, very
slightly. As if he knew exactly what

401
00:31:11,701 --> 00:31:14,355
she was thinking, and found it charming.

402
00:31:15,924 --> 00:31:21,842
Evelyn Marsh did not clap either. She was not
suspicious. She simply forgot to. She was

403
00:31:21,862 --> 00:31:27,848
watching him, and thinking, with the absolute clarity
of a young woman who has spent her

404
00:31:27,868 --> 00:31:33,717
whole life being overlooked, that she had never in
her life seen anyone win like that.

405
00:31:35,788 --> 00:31:39,817
That night, in the empty building, a new
panel opened in the air.

406
00:31:41,135 --> 00:31:42,750
The Shop is unlocked.

407
00:31:47,871 --> 00:31:53,684
And Albert Wesker, who had once been a god in his own
estimation, and Adolf, who had once

408
00:31:53,704 --> 00:31:59,452
been the master of a continent, sat down together in
a creaking leather chair — and went

409
00:32:13,925 --> 00:32:15,931
The Shop did not look like a shop.

410
00:32:16,501 --> 00:32:22,625
It looked like a war room drawn in light. Panel
after panel unfolded in the dark of the

411
00:32:22,645 --> 00:32:29,194
corner office, each one ruled with thin crimson lines,
each one scrolling quietly, patiently,

412
00:32:29,214 --> 00:32:35,480
like a catalogue written by something that had all
the time in the world and knew exactly

413
00:32:35,500 --> 00:32:36,540
what he wanted.

414
00:32:37,860 --> 00:32:39,089
Facilities.

415
00:32:46,754 --> 00:32:52,787
A containment laboratory that would appear,
overnight, behind a wall that had always been

416
00:32:52,807 --> 00:32:58,837
there. An entire underground level, drawn in glowing
wireframe beneath the building, with

417
00:32:58,857 --> 00:33:01,421
a price attached like a tag on a coat.

418
00:33:02,741 --> 00:33:08,705
Personnel. Personnel acquired through the
System cannot disobey.

419
00:33:11,455 --> 00:33:13,369
That line he read three times.

420
00:33:13,940 --> 00:33:20,472
Cannot disobey. Not will not. Cannot. Human beings —
or things shaped exactly like them

421
00:33:20,492 --> 00:33:27,100
— delivered with their loyalty already installed, the
way a car arrives with its engine.

422
00:33:27,791 --> 00:33:33,575
A guard. Buy a guard. Every movement begins with
men who will stand at the door.

423
00:33:34,095 --> 00:33:40,040
Every movement you started ended with men standing at a
door in a bunker. Let's try something

424
00:33:40,060 --> 00:33:40,298
new.

425
00:33:41,446 --> 00:33:43,142
Powers and immunities.

426
00:33:48,946 --> 00:33:51,976
Viral. Prototype stabilization.

427
00:33:57,541 --> 00:34:03,423
He had seventeen hundred Points. The stability
meter in the corner of his vision read

428
00:34:03,443 --> 00:34:04,743
sixty-five percent.

429
00:34:06,312 --> 00:34:11,835
He wanted everything. That was the first honest
feeling he had in the Shop, and it was

430
00:34:11,855 --> 00:34:17,572
so strong it was almost physical. He wanted the
containment lab and the underground level

431
00:34:17,592 --> 00:34:23,114
and the guard and the immunity and the power to read
a man's face like a newspaper. He

432
00:34:23,134 --> 00:34:28,721
wanted, if he was honest, to buy the whole catalogue
in one night and wake up on Monday

433
00:34:28,742 --> 00:34:29,172
a king.

434
00:34:29,742 --> 00:34:33,742
Instead, he bought the cheapest thing on the
list that would keep him alive.

435
00:34:35,063 --> 00:34:38,855
Purchase confirmed. Viral decay halted.

436
00:34:40,583 --> 00:34:46,523
He felt it immediately. A tightness under the
sternum he had not known was there, like

437
00:34:46,543 --> 00:34:52,550
a fist he had been holding for five days, opened.
The virus in his blood did not become

438
00:34:52,570 --> 00:34:54,699
tame. It simply stopped eating.

439
00:34:55,269 --> 00:35:01,802
It was, the narrator is obliged to record, the only
time in this entire story that Albert

440
00:35:01,822 --> 00:35:04,157
Wesker spent Points on humility.

441
00:35:05,849 --> 00:35:07,021
And the rest?

442
00:35:07,541 --> 00:35:10,380
The rest buys the most dangerous
man in the building.

443
00:35:10,900 --> 00:35:12,117
A soldier.

444
00:35:12,639 --> 00:35:13,597
An accountant.

445
00:35:15,248 --> 00:35:21,324
Purchase confirmed. Comptroller.
Arrival, Monday, eight A M.

446
00:35:22,913 --> 00:35:25,753
You have spent our fortune on a clerk.

447
00:35:26,271 --> 00:35:31,621
Money is the only army that works in peacetime.
And I've just hired its general.

448
00:35:33,641 --> 00:35:39,884
On Monday morning, at eight o'clock exactly, a man
named Mister Pell walked into Division

449
00:35:39,904 --> 00:35:41,925
Seven and asked for his desk.

450
00:35:42,494 --> 00:35:48,391
He was thin and pale, somewhere in his fifties, in a
grey suit so neat it looked ironed

451
00:35:48,411 --> 00:35:54,306
onto him. He carried a leather briefcase and a paper
bag containing, it would turn out,

452
00:35:54,326 --> 00:36:00,221
the same lunch he would eat every day for the next
thirteen years: a cheese sandwich on

453
00:36:00,240 --> 00:36:03,688
white bread, an apple, and a
bottle of still water.

454
00:36:04,259 --> 00:36:08,636
Dana looked him up in the payroll system,
because she had never heard of him.

455
00:36:09,206 --> 00:36:14,943
He was there. Hired in March. Chartered accountant,
certified in three states, with a

456
00:36:14,963 --> 00:36:20,768
clean background check, a signed contract, a parking
space with his name painted on it

457
00:36:20,788 --> 00:36:26,592
that she could have sworn had been blank on Friday,
and a performance review from June

458
00:36:26,612 --> 00:36:31,875
that she did not remember writing, but that was
unmistakably in her own words.

459
00:36:32,563 --> 00:36:36,268
I — sorry. I'm so sorry, Mister Pell. I
don't know why I didn't —

460
00:36:36,788 --> 00:36:41,056
It's quite all right, Mrs. Whitfield. It's been
a difficult month for everyone.

461
00:36:42,177 --> 00:36:47,717
His voice was pleasant. His manners were perfect. He
remembered her name, and her late

462
00:36:47,737 --> 00:36:53,409
son's name, and the fact that she took her coffee
with oat milk. And there was something

463
00:36:53,429 --> 00:36:58,905
about the way he stood — absolutely still, his
weight exactly even on both feet, like

464
00:36:58,925 --> 00:37:03,369
a coat on a hanger — that made her want,
very slightly, to step back.

465
00:37:03,938 --> 00:37:08,583
She told herself it was nothing. She would tell
herself that for a long time.

466
00:37:10,152 --> 00:37:11,382
Pell went to work.

467
00:37:11,952 --> 00:37:18,134
By lunchtime on Monday he had found a claim that
Division Seven held against its own dead

468
00:37:18,154 --> 00:37:24,407
parent company — six years of unpaid intercompany
charges, two hundred and ninety thousand

469
00:37:24,427 --> 00:37:30,400
dollars that Umbrella Holdings in London had owed to
Umbrella Delaware and never paid,

470
00:37:30,420 --> 00:37:36,463
now filed as a creditor claim with Harrow and Lisle,
ahead of half the other creditors.

471
00:37:37,032 --> 00:37:42,693
By Tuesday he had found a research tax credit the
division had been entitled to since

472
00:37:42,712 --> 00:37:47,637
two thousand nineteen and had never claimed,
because nobody had known how.

473
00:37:48,208 --> 00:37:54,252
By Wednesday he had renegotiated the electricity
contract, the waste-disposal contract,

474
00:37:54,273 --> 00:37:56,833
the equipment leases, and the coffee.

475
00:37:57,402 --> 00:38:03,320
He did not raise his voice once. He did not seem to
get tired. When Tomás asked him, as

476
00:38:03,340 --> 00:38:09,190
a joke, whether he ever went home, Pell looked at
him with polite puzzlement, as if he

477
00:38:09,210 --> 00:38:12,603
had been asked whether he ever
turned into a bird.

478
00:38:13,293 --> 00:38:14,882
I live quite close.

479
00:38:15,402 --> 00:38:17,389
Nobody ever found out where.

480
00:38:19,157 --> 00:38:24,519
There was one other thing that happened that week,
and it happened quietly, and nobody

481
00:38:24,539 --> 00:38:27,837
except Albert Wesker understood how
much it mattered.

482
00:38:28,407 --> 00:38:34,710
On Tuesday morning, the Director had handed Tomás a
sample tube with a white label that

483
00:38:34,730 --> 00:38:40,887
said, in neat handwriting, Control Seven, and asked
him to run the full panel on it —

484
00:38:40,907 --> 00:38:45,103
to calibrate the analyzers, he said,
after the power cuts.

485
00:38:45,673 --> 00:38:47,228
It was his own blood.

486
00:38:47,798 --> 00:38:54,208
The analyzer had not liked it. It flagged a
white-cell count it described as implausible,

487
00:38:54,228 --> 00:39:00,855
a division rate it described as impossible, and a viral
signature it could not match against

488
00:39:00,875 --> 00:39:06,563
anything in its library. It printed three pages
of errors, and then it crashed.

489
00:39:07,253 --> 00:39:12,378
Yeah, so — Control Seven broke the machine. I think
the reagents are bad. I'll re-order.

490
00:39:12,898 --> 00:39:16,121
Don't. Throw the printout away. The
reagents are fine.

491
00:39:17,239 --> 00:39:19,197
Tomás threw the printout away.

492
00:39:19,768 --> 00:39:25,194
And at six fifteen that evening, Doctor Howard
Lessing, who had spent thirty years going

493
00:39:25,215 --> 00:39:30,643
through other people's garbage looking for the thing
that would finally make him famous,

494
00:39:30,663 --> 00:39:32,067
took it out of the bin.

495
00:39:33,635 --> 00:39:37,231
He read it standing up. Then he sat
down and read it again.

496
00:39:37,802 --> 00:39:43,668
He did not know whose blood it was. He did not know
what the signature meant. But he knew,

497
00:39:43,688 --> 00:39:49,492
with a certainty that made his hands cold, that he
was looking at something no human cell

498
00:39:49,511 --> 00:39:55,313
was supposed to do — cells dividing and repairing at
a rate that should have been cancer,

499
00:39:55,333 --> 00:40:00,875
and was not. A viral genome that the database did
not recognise, because the database

500
00:40:00,894 --> 00:40:03,360
had never seen it. Because nobody had.

501
00:40:04,050 --> 00:40:05,010
My God.

502
00:40:05,530 --> 00:40:11,197
That night he stayed late. He told his wife he was
running an overnight assay. He went

503
00:40:11,217 --> 00:40:16,884
into the analyzer's memory, where the raw data from
Control Seven was still sitting in

504
00:40:16,904 --> 00:40:22,637
a folder nobody had thought to delete, and he copied
it onto a small blue drive that he

505
00:40:22,657 --> 00:40:24,027
kept on his keychain.

506
00:40:24,594 --> 00:40:30,094
Then he put the drive in his pocket, and drove home
very carefully, the way a man drives

507
00:40:30,114 --> 00:40:33,920
when he believes he is carrying his
whole future in his coat.

508
00:40:35,989 --> 00:40:41,831
The Director, of course, already knew. But that week he
was busy with something more interesting

509
00:40:41,851 --> 00:40:43,054
than Howard Lessing.

510
00:40:43,623 --> 00:40:45,277
He was busy with himself.

511
00:40:45,847 --> 00:40:51,467
Every night, after the building emptied, he took
the old microscope in lab three, and

512
00:40:51,487 --> 00:40:54,586
a fresh sample of his own blood,
and he looked.

513
00:40:55,155 --> 00:41:00,940
He had done this before, in another life. In another
world he had watched this same virus

514
00:41:00,961 --> 00:41:06,550
under far better lenses, in laboratories deep
beneath the Arklay Mountains, built with

515
00:41:06,569 --> 00:41:12,288
a budget that could have bought this whole city. But
he had never looked at it like this

516
00:41:12,309 --> 00:41:14,766
— from inside the body it had rebuilt.

517
00:41:15,337 --> 00:41:21,168
The Prototype was beautiful. That was the Wesker
half's opinion, and he did not hold it

518
00:41:21,188 --> 00:41:27,422
lightly. It moved through the sample like a patient
army, finding damaged cells and rewriting

519
00:41:27,442 --> 00:41:33,271
them, finding healthy cells and improving them,
never wasting anything, never hurrying.

520
00:41:33,291 --> 00:41:38,987
Everything it touched became stronger. Everything
that could not be made stronger, it

521
00:41:39,007 --> 00:41:39,594
consumed.

522
00:41:41,083 --> 00:41:43,461
It is like an idea.

523
00:41:43,980 --> 00:41:44,942
It's a virus.

524
00:41:45,461 --> 00:41:52,023
It is like an idea. It enters. It finds what is
weak. It rewrites it, or it removes it,

525
00:41:52,043 --> 00:41:55,958
and everything that remains is
stronger than before.

526
00:41:56,478 --> 00:41:58,827
You're describing a disease, you realise.

527
00:41:59,346 --> 00:42:01,779
I am describing a nation.

528
00:42:03,300 --> 00:42:09,029
Wesker did not answer. In the dark lab, the man at
the microscope sat very still, while

529
00:42:09,050 --> 00:42:14,645
two minds shared one set of eyes, and looked at the
same thing, and saw it completely

530
00:42:14,666 --> 00:42:20,395
differently. And the most frightening thing about
the next fifty years was that, in the

531
00:42:20,416 --> 00:42:23,632
end, it would not matter which of
them was right.

532
00:42:25,702 --> 00:42:29,900
At three in the morning, walking back to his
office, he heard a piano.

533
00:42:30,467 --> 00:42:35,963
There was one in the break room. Nobody knew where
it had come from. It had been there

534
00:42:35,983 --> 00:42:41,608
since the building was built, badly out of tune, and
Tomás occasionally played the first

535
00:42:41,628 --> 00:42:44,815
half of a video game theme on it
to annoy Lessing.

536
00:42:45,385 --> 00:42:51,630
Evelyn Marsh was sitting at it, alone, in her lab
coat, playing four notes over and over.

537
00:42:51,650 --> 00:42:56,416
Falling, and falling, and falling, and
landing somewhere unresolved.

538
00:42:57,385 --> 00:43:03,121
It was not a tune he knew. And yet it was, somehow,
exactly the sound of the building.

539
00:43:03,141 --> 00:43:09,077
Of the rain, and the freezers, and the dead company,
and whatever it was about to become.

540
00:43:09,766 --> 00:43:11,338
You're here late, Doctor Marsh.

541
00:43:12,159 --> 00:43:16,045
She jumped a little. Then she didn't
apologise, which he noticed.

542
00:43:16,733 --> 00:43:21,855
So are you. I couldn't sleep. The sequencer's free
at night. Are you — can I ask what

543
00:43:21,875 --> 00:43:25,179
you're working on? Every night. The
light in lab three.

544
00:43:25,699 --> 00:43:28,559
Something that will make your paper
look like a footnote.

545
00:43:29,679 --> 00:43:30,556
Can I help?

546
00:43:31,077 --> 00:43:36,961
He looked at her for a long moment. The Wesker half
of him saw a talented young woman

547
00:43:36,981 --> 00:43:43,074
with no loyalties, no family nearby, nothing to lose,
and an enormous hunger to be seen.

548
00:43:43,094 --> 00:43:49,396
The other half saw something else. Something it had
seen before, in other rooms, in another

549
00:43:49,416 --> 00:43:52,731
century, in the faces of young
people in crowds.

550
00:43:53,300 --> 00:43:55,307
Both halves agreed on the answer.

551
00:43:55,996 --> 00:43:56,755
Not yet.

552
00:43:58,775 --> 00:44:04,608
On Friday morning, at one minute to eight, Mister
Pell knocked on the door of the corner

553
00:44:04,627 --> 00:44:10,458
office with a single sheet of paper in his hand. He
read it aloud without looking at it,

554
00:44:10,478 --> 00:44:13,782
in a voice as flat and pleasant as
a hotel carpet.

555
00:44:14,472 --> 00:44:19,219
Division Seven's cash position is now four hundred
and eighty-one thousand dollars, with

556
00:44:19,239 --> 00:44:24,255
a further two hundred and ninety thousand receivable
from the London administration, expected

557
00:44:24,275 --> 00:44:29,076
in the first quarter. Operating costs have been
reduced by nineteen percent. The research

558
00:44:29,096 --> 00:44:34,059
tax credit claim has been filed. I've moved the coffee
to a different supplier. It's better.

559
00:44:34,578 --> 00:44:35,145
Good.

560
00:44:35,666 --> 00:44:37,907
There is one problem, Director.

561
00:44:39,027 --> 00:44:39,742
Go on.

562
00:44:55,842 --> 00:44:58,821
The problem with the landlord
was a paragraph.

563
00:44:59,511 --> 00:45:04,487
Clause fourteen of the building lease, Director. A
change-of-control provision. When the

564
00:45:04,507 --> 00:45:09,425
ownership of the tenant changes, the landlord may
terminate on sixty days' notice. Your

565
00:45:09,445 --> 00:45:14,306
purchase of the division last week was a change of
control. Mister Kuchar has noticed.

566
00:45:14,826 --> 00:45:16,654
Mister Kuchar reads leases?

567
00:45:17,175 --> 00:45:22,008
Mister Kuchar does not. But someone has read it for
him. On Tuesday, a property agent

568
00:45:22,028 --> 00:45:27,147
acting for Verdant Axis Therapeutics offered him
double the current rent for the building,

569
00:45:27,167 --> 00:45:28,347
starting in December.

570
00:45:29,667 --> 00:45:32,853
So Graham Tolliver had finally
read page three.

571
00:45:33,423 --> 00:45:39,225
He had read the word non-exclusive. He had understood
that he had paid one point two million

572
00:45:39,245 --> 00:45:44,667
dollars of his shareholders' money for a thing that
Umbrella could sell again tomorrow

573
00:45:44,687 --> 00:45:50,236
to anyone it liked. And he had understood that if he
said so to his board, he would have

574
00:45:50,256 --> 00:45:52,261
to explain why he had signed it.

575
00:45:52,832 --> 00:45:57,500
So he had said nothing. He had simply decided
to take the building instead.

576
00:45:58,189 --> 00:46:02,889
He is attacking the ground beneath your
feet. It is what I would do.

577
00:46:03,410 --> 00:46:07,465
It's what a child would do. He's too angry
to be clever. That's useful.

578
00:46:08,863 --> 00:46:10,892
Thank you, Mister Pell. Leave it with me.

579
00:46:11,411 --> 00:46:13,205
Of course, Director.

580
00:46:13,726 --> 00:46:18,356
Pell left. He closed the door so quietly
that the latch did not click.

581
00:46:20,124 --> 00:46:24,267
The System paid him for Graham Tolliver's
silence on the drive home.

582
00:46:24,836 --> 00:46:30,801
He had stopped at a red light on Market Street, with
the wipers going and the radio off,

583
00:46:30,821 --> 00:46:36,855
when the air above the steering wheel lit up and
began, very neatly, to itemise his week.

584
00:46:38,175 --> 00:46:41,782
Assessment. Total, three thousand Points.

585
00:46:50,085 --> 00:46:52,155
Level up. Level three.

586
00:46:54,389 --> 00:47:00,626
He put three points into Charisma, and two into
Special. He wanted to know what the second

587
00:47:00,646 --> 00:47:03,893
one did. The System, as usual,
did not explain.

588
00:47:04,463 --> 00:47:10,174
The light turned green. He did not move. A car behind
him honked, and then thought better

589
00:47:10,194 --> 00:47:11,655
of it, and went around.

590
00:47:13,375 --> 00:47:21,022
Draw. Standard pool. One thousand Points per
draw. Rewards are randomised.

591
00:47:22,612 --> 00:47:28,100
It had been there since the Shop unlocked. A second
tab, beside the catalogue, that he

592
00:47:28,120 --> 00:47:30,405
had been very carefully not opening.

593
00:47:31,695 --> 00:47:32,741
Open it.

594
00:47:33,261 --> 00:47:34,455
It's a slot machine.

595
00:47:34,975 --> 00:47:40,566
It is fate. You bought your life, and you bought a
clerk. You have never once in this

596
00:47:40,586 --> 00:47:41,952
world let fate speak.

597
00:47:42,473 --> 00:47:48,530
Fate is what losers call variance. You know what
variance does to a man with three thousand

598
00:47:48,550 --> 00:47:51,669
Points? It turns him into a man
with no Points.

599
00:47:52,188 --> 00:47:58,494
I crossed a border in nineteen thirty-six with
three battalions and orders to retreat

600
00:47:58,514 --> 00:48:05,043
if the French so much as fired. They did not fire.
Everything after began with that roll

601
00:48:05,063 --> 00:48:05,936
of the dice.

602
00:48:06,457 --> 00:48:08,592
And everything after ended in a bunker.

603
00:48:09,111 --> 00:48:10,883
But not at first.

604
00:48:12,604 --> 00:48:14,366
The rain drummed on the roof.

605
00:48:14,937 --> 00:48:21,269
And the Wesker half of the mind — the scientist, the
cynic, the man who had despised gamblers

606
00:48:21,289 --> 00:48:26,938
all his life — found, with considerable
irritation, that he wanted to press it too.

607
00:48:27,627 --> 00:48:28,267
Once.

608
00:48:33,118 --> 00:48:35,322
Common. Maintenance technician.

609
00:48:39,764 --> 00:48:40,839
A janitor.

610
00:48:41,409 --> 00:48:47,114
He had gambled a thousand Points on the wheel of
fate, and fate had given him a man with

611
00:48:47,134 --> 00:48:47,504
a mop.

612
00:48:48,194 --> 00:48:49,198
Again.

613
00:48:49,718 --> 00:48:50,869
Absolutely not.

614
00:48:51,390 --> 00:48:52,348
Again.

615
00:48:53,668 --> 00:48:54,848
He drew again.

616
00:48:59,368 --> 00:49:02,177
Rare. Microexpression cold-read.

617
00:49:06,643 --> 00:49:08,446
It went in through the eyes.

618
00:49:09,016 --> 00:49:14,091
There was a pressure behind them, and then a
headache like a nail, and then — nothing.

619
00:49:14,111 --> 00:49:16,225
The rain. The wipers. The dashboard.

620
00:49:16,795 --> 00:49:21,920
He did not understand what he had bought until he
looked in the rear-view mirror at his

621
00:49:21,940 --> 00:49:26,060
own face, and read it, the way he might have
read a paragraph of text.

622
00:49:26,629 --> 00:49:31,281
Contempt. Curiosity. And underneath, bright
as a warning light, hunger.

623
00:49:32,970 --> 00:49:33,855
That's enough.

624
00:49:34,374 --> 00:49:35,472
One more.

625
00:49:35,992 --> 00:49:37,145
I said that's enough.

626
00:49:37,665 --> 00:49:43,970
You have thirteen hundred Points and a clerk and a
janitor and a party trick. One more.

627
00:49:43,990 --> 00:49:46,732
And then I will not ask again tonight.

628
00:49:48,752 --> 00:49:54,638
Later — years later — when people asked Albert
Wesker whether he believed in destiny,

629
00:49:54,658 --> 00:50:00,613
he would smile and say that he believed in
statistics. It was a good line. It was also

630
00:50:00,633 --> 00:50:07,143
a lie, because he could remember the exact moment he had
stopped believing only in statistics.

631
00:50:07,713 --> 00:50:13,110
It was a red light on Market Street, in the rain, at
twenty minutes past eight, when he

632
00:50:13,130 --> 00:50:15,040
pressed the draw one more time.

633
00:50:18,610 --> 00:50:19,996
The light was gold.

634
00:50:20,566 --> 00:50:26,040
It filled the inside of the car. It was so bright
that for an instant he was sure the

635
00:50:26,060 --> 00:50:31,534
people in the other cars could see it, that the
whole of Market Street was lit like a

636
00:50:31,554 --> 00:50:31,922
stage.

637
00:50:33,242 --> 00:50:37,365
Legendary. Biological asset. Tyrant.

638
00:50:42,841 --> 00:50:46,701
And for half a second — no more —
Albert Wesker was afraid.

639
00:50:47,271 --> 00:50:53,451
He knew what a Tyrant was. In his old world he had
helped design them. Eight feet of engineered

640
00:50:53,471 --> 00:50:59,064
flesh, a heart that could keep beating through a
rocket strike, hands that could put a

641
00:50:59,084 --> 00:51:04,677
fist through a steel door and a man through a wall.
A weapon that walked. A thing that

642
00:51:04,697 --> 00:51:10,225
did not stop. He had seen what one could do to a
room full of trained soldiers in the

643
00:51:10,245 --> 00:51:12,509
time it took them to decide to run.

644
00:51:13,079 --> 00:51:19,450
And it was his. Asleep, in the dark, under a building
on the wrong side of an industrial

645
00:51:19,470 --> 00:51:21,048
park in Delaware. His.

646
00:51:22,617 --> 00:51:27,761
The fear lasted half a second. What replaced
it lasted a great deal longer.

647
00:51:28,451 --> 00:51:29,306
Fate.

648
00:51:29,826 --> 00:51:30,657
Variance.

649
00:51:31,175 --> 00:51:33,074
But he was smiling. They both were.

650
00:51:35,644 --> 00:51:39,384
Gus arrived at six the next morning,
with his own keys.

651
00:51:39,954 --> 00:51:45,571
He was stocky and quiet, somewhere in his fifties,
in navy coveralls with a tool belt

652
00:51:45,591 --> 00:51:51,207
and a name tag that had been sewn on long enough to
fade. He knew where every duct in

653
00:51:51,227 --> 00:51:56,910
the building went. He knew which fuse tripped when
the autoclave and the kettle ran at

654
00:51:56,930 --> 00:52:02,811
the same time. He knew the code to the basement door,
which had been changed on Thursday.

655
00:52:03,381 --> 00:52:08,757
When Walter Pruitt came to relieve the weekend
guard, he found Gus up a ladder on the

656
00:52:08,777 --> 00:52:14,343
second floor, replacing a tube that had flickered for
three years, and said good morning

657
00:52:14,363 --> 00:52:18,978
to him the way you say good morning to a man
you have known for a decade.

658
00:52:19,547 --> 00:52:25,233
It was only on the drive home that Walter realised
he had never seen him before in his

659
00:52:25,253 --> 00:52:25,565
life.

660
00:52:27,254 --> 00:52:27,819
Huh.

661
00:52:28,338 --> 00:52:33,671
He thought about it for a long time. Then, being the
kind of man he was, he decided it

662
00:52:33,691 --> 00:52:36,285
was none of his business, and went to bed.

663
00:52:38,055 --> 00:52:39,947
The Tyrant was in the basement.

664
00:52:40,518 --> 00:52:46,134
That is to say — the thing it slept in was in the
basement, and the thing it slept in

665
00:52:46,154 --> 00:52:51,969
looked, to anyone who did not know better, exactly
like an industrial air-handling unit.

666
00:52:52,538 --> 00:52:58,535
It stood in the corner beyond the freezers, sixteen
feet tall and painted a dull, municipal

667
00:52:58,555 --> 00:53:04,154
grey, with ducting that ran up into the ceiling and
a riveted service plate that read

668
00:53:04,174 --> 00:53:09,840
Arcturus Climate Systems, Model T X zero zero. It
was bolted to the floor with anchors

669
00:53:09,860 --> 00:53:15,592
that looked twenty years old. It had a maintenance
log in a plastic sleeve on its side,

670
00:53:15,612 --> 00:53:21,211
and the log was filled in, in Gus's handwriting,
going back to two thousand and nine.

671
00:53:21,782 --> 00:53:23,020
And it breathed.

672
00:53:23,589 --> 00:53:29,748
Every six seconds, somewhere deep inside it, a
compressor cycled. A long, slow draw. A

673
00:53:29,768 --> 00:53:36,431
pause. A long, slow release. To anyone who walked past,
it was machinery. To the man standing

674
00:53:36,451 --> 00:53:42,539
in front of it that Saturday morning, it was the
sound of something enormous, asleep.

675
00:53:44,110 --> 00:53:49,934
There was a small inspection window on its side, the
size of a paperback, frosted white.

676
00:53:50,504 --> 00:53:52,776
He did not open it. Not yet.

677
00:53:54,546 --> 00:53:56,629
Dale Kuchar came on Tuesday.

678
00:53:57,198 --> 00:54:03,173
He was sixty, heavyset, in a windbreaker and a
Phillies cap, with a clipboard he had bought

679
00:54:03,193 --> 00:54:08,772
specifically for this visit and a printout of the
building's electric bill, which had

680
00:54:08,792 --> 00:54:12,592
gone up by a figure he had circled
three times in red pen.

681
00:54:13,283 --> 00:54:17,534
I'm just saying, Doc, that's a lot of juice. I get
the bill. My name's on the meter. And

682
00:54:17,554 --> 00:54:19,961
I got a right under the lease to
inspect for, uh —

683
00:54:20,480 --> 00:54:22,013
He looked at his clipboard.

684
00:54:22,703 --> 00:54:25,597
— for unauthorised alterations
to the premises.

685
00:54:26,118 --> 00:54:31,346
Of course, Mister Kuchar. Inspect whatever you
like. Gus will show you the basement.

686
00:54:32,365 --> 00:54:37,910
Kuchar went down to the basement with Gus. He came
back up twenty minutes later with the

687
00:54:37,930 --> 00:54:43,286
expression of a man who has been shown a magic
trick, and is trying very hard to work

688
00:54:43,306 --> 00:54:44,803
out where the card went.

689
00:54:45,494 --> 00:54:47,992
That unit down there. The big grey
one. That's new.

690
00:54:48,512 --> 00:54:51,257
Been here since oh-nine, Mister Kuchar.

691
00:54:51,777 --> 00:54:55,919
It has not been there since oh-nine. I been down
there. I did the flood in twenty-one.

692
00:54:55,939 --> 00:54:56,984
That corner was empty.

693
00:54:57,504 --> 00:54:59,610
It's in the log, Mister Kuchar.

694
00:55:00,730 --> 00:55:07,499
Gus said it with no emphasis at all. Not defensive.
Not amused. The way a clock says it's

695
00:55:07,519 --> 00:55:08,339
ten-thirty.

696
00:55:08,908 --> 00:55:14,481
And Albert Wesker, standing in the lobby with his
hands folded behind his back, watched

697
00:55:14,501 --> 00:55:18,532
the landlord's face — and for the first
time, he could read it.

698
00:55:19,100 --> 00:55:25,230
The narrowing around the eyes: suspicion. The
tightening at the corner of the mouth: greed.

699
00:55:25,250 --> 00:55:30,974
The quick, involuntary glance toward the exit,
there and gone in a fifth of a second:

700
00:55:30,994 --> 00:55:36,854
fear. Dale Kuchar did not know what he had seen in
the basement. But some very old part

701
00:55:36,874 --> 00:55:39,624
of his brain did, and it wanted to leave.

702
00:55:40,195 --> 00:55:46,150
And beneath all three, clear as print: he had
already made up his mind to take Verdant

703
00:55:46,170 --> 00:55:47,054
Axis's money.

704
00:55:47,744 --> 00:55:49,858
Was there anything else, Mister Kuchar?

705
00:55:50,376 --> 00:55:52,463
I'm gonna — yeah. I'm gonna be in touch.

706
00:55:52,985 --> 00:55:58,396
On his way out, standing in the parking lot, he took
a photograph of the building on his

707
00:55:58,416 --> 00:56:01,359
phone, as if he wanted proof it
was still there.

708
00:56:03,428 --> 00:56:05,950
The Cold-Read found other
things that week.

709
00:56:06,519 --> 00:56:12,575
It found that Dana Whitfield had started to trust
him, and was ashamed of how much. It

710
00:56:12,595 --> 00:56:18,720
found that Tomás Reyes wanted, more than anything,
to be told he was good at something.

711
00:56:18,740 --> 00:56:23,807
It found that Nadia Okafor was still
measuring the distance to the door.

712
00:56:24,376 --> 00:56:26,060
And it found Howard Lessing.

713
00:56:26,629 --> 00:56:32,411
It found him in the corridor on Wednesday, coming
out of lab two with a coffee, saying

714
00:56:32,431 --> 00:56:38,213
good morning without looking up. Guilt, the
Cold-Read said, in the tiny tension of the

715
00:56:38,233 --> 00:56:44,014
muscles around his eyes. Guilt, and fear — and
underneath them, like a coal under ash,

716
00:56:44,034 --> 00:56:44,486
hunger.

717
00:56:45,057 --> 00:56:50,753
Lessing's right hand was in his coat pocket. It was
closed around his keys. And on his

718
00:56:50,773 --> 00:56:56,602
key ring, the Director noticed, there was a small
blue drive that had not been there the

719
00:56:56,622 --> 00:56:57,399
week before.

720
00:56:58,089 --> 00:56:59,818
He has taken something.

721
00:57:00,339 --> 00:57:02,000
He's taken Control Seven.

722
00:57:02,519 --> 00:57:07,349
Then he is a thief. And a thief in a
small kingdom is a traitor.

723
00:57:07,869 --> 00:57:11,241
A thief is an opportunity. He just
doesn't know it yet.

724
00:57:13,260 --> 00:57:19,263
On Thursday night, at three in the morning, when even
Evelyn Marsh had gone home, Albert

725
00:57:19,283 --> 00:57:21,932
Wesker went down to the basement alone.

726
00:57:22,503 --> 00:57:27,326
He stood in front of the grey machine for a long
time, listening to it breathe.

727
00:57:27,895 --> 00:57:33,288
Then he took off his glove, and laid his bare hand
flat against the frosted inspection

728
00:57:33,308 --> 00:57:33,729
window.

729
00:57:34,298 --> 00:57:40,272
The frost was so cold it burned. He didn't move his
hand. And after a while the heat of

730
00:57:40,292 --> 00:57:46,196
his palm made a small, clear circle in the ice, and
through it, in the dark inside the

731
00:57:46,216 --> 00:57:47,988
machine, he saw something.

732
00:57:49,557 --> 00:57:50,773
It was an eye.

733
00:57:51,342 --> 00:57:57,071
It was pale, and enormous, and it was open. It
had not been open a moment before.

734
00:57:59,140 --> 00:58:05,128
It looked at him through the glass. It did not blink.
And somewhere inside the unit, too

735
00:58:05,148 --> 00:58:10,999
low to be a sound so much as a pressure in the
concrete, something that had never been

736
00:58:11,019 --> 00:58:13,797
born, and was his, recognised its master.

737
00:58:14,488 --> 00:58:15,321
Not yet.

738
00:58:16,841 --> 00:58:18,038
The eye closed.

739
00:58:18,608 --> 00:58:22,356
The compressor cycled. A long, slow
breath. A pause.

740
00:58:22,926 --> 00:58:28,361
And the most dangerous thing in the state of Delaware
went back to sleep in its box, and

741
00:58:28,381 --> 00:58:33,879
dreamed whatever things like that dream about,
beneath a building whose landlord had just

742
00:58:50,241 --> 00:58:56,617
The solution to Dale Kuchar arrived, like most of
Mister Pell's solutions, in three documents

743
00:58:56,637 --> 00:58:59,231
laid out in a perfectly straight line.

744
00:58:59,922 --> 00:59:04,965
The first is an email from Shenzhen, Director. The
contract research company that asked

745
00:59:04,985 --> 00:59:08,166
about the archive in August. They
are still interested.

746
00:59:08,686 --> 00:59:09,797
Of course they are.

747
00:59:10,315 --> 00:59:15,389
The second is a licence agreement. Non-exclusive.
One point five million dollars. They

748
00:59:15,409 --> 00:59:17,936
have indicated they will sign
it this week.

749
00:59:18,956 --> 00:59:25,016
Graham Tolliver had been terrified that Umbrella
would sell his archive to the Chinese.

750
00:59:25,036 --> 00:59:28,159
It had not been true. By
Friday, it would be.

751
00:59:28,730 --> 00:59:34,250
There is a kind of cruelty that only exists in
business, where you punish a man simply

752
00:59:34,269 --> 00:59:36,438
by making his worst fear accurate.

753
00:59:37,130 --> 00:59:41,764
The third is a purchase agreement for the building.
Two point nine million. One point

754
00:59:41,784 --> 00:59:46,418
five down, the rest carried by the seller over five
years. Mister Kuchar owes a great

755
00:59:46,438 --> 00:59:51,509
deal of money on his other properties. Verdant Axis has
offered him rent. We will be offering

756
00:59:51,528 --> 00:59:52,275
him a way out.

757
00:59:52,795 --> 00:59:53,713
And the clause?

758
00:59:54,235 --> 00:59:59,541
Section nine. A right of first refusal on the two
adjoining lots he owns in the park.

759
01:00:00,561 --> 01:00:01,585
He won't read it.

760
01:00:02,105 --> 01:00:05,788
He has not read anything since nineteen
ninety-four, Director.

761
01:00:07,507 --> 01:00:12,806
They met at a diner on the edge of the park, because
Kuchar did not want to go back into

762
01:00:12,826 --> 01:00:13,592
the building.

763
01:00:14,163 --> 01:00:19,730
He had not slept well since his visit to the
basement. He did not know why. He told his

764
01:00:19,750 --> 01:00:21,400
wife it was the heartburn.

765
01:00:22,088 --> 01:00:23,365
Two point nine.

766
01:00:23,885 --> 01:00:24,889
Two point nine.

767
01:00:25,409 --> 01:00:27,284
Verdant's offering double rent.

768
01:00:27,804 --> 01:00:33,020
Verdant is offering you rent, Mister Kuchar, on a
building you'll still own, with a roof

769
01:00:33,041 --> 01:00:38,199
that leaks and a boiler that's older than your
grandson, while your bank is calling you

770
01:00:38,219 --> 01:00:41,889
about the property on Heald Street every
Tuesday and Thursday.

771
01:00:43,009 --> 01:00:49,326
Kuchar's face went through three expressions in half a
second. The Director read all three.

772
01:00:49,346 --> 01:00:55,248
Shock. Shame. And then — relief, the particular
relief of a drowning man who has been

773
01:00:55,268 --> 01:00:57,686
told exactly how deep the water is.

774
01:00:58,376 --> 01:01:00,208
How do you know about Heald Street?

775
01:01:00,730 --> 01:01:05,061
I read. Two point nine, and you never have to
walk into that basement again.

776
01:01:06,581 --> 01:01:07,635
He signed.

777
01:01:08,206 --> 01:01:13,742
He did not read section nine. He did not, in fact,
read anything past the number on the

778
01:01:13,762 --> 01:01:19,237
first page. He signed where the little yellow flags
told him to sign, and he shook the

779
01:01:19,257 --> 01:01:25,112
Director's hand, and he drove away from the industrial
park for the last time with a feeling

780
01:01:25,132 --> 01:01:29,838
that he would describe to his wife that
evening as having dodged a bullet.

781
01:01:30,409 --> 01:01:32,154
He was more right than he knew.

782
01:01:34,474 --> 01:01:40,449
Assessment. Territory secured. Two
thousand six hundred Points.

783
01:01:42,516 --> 01:01:47,784
Territory, the System called it. Not property.
Not real estate. Territory.

784
01:01:48,355 --> 01:01:52,952
The Adolf half of the mind noticed that
word, and liked it very much.

785
01:01:54,724 --> 01:01:56,556
That night he bought the guard.

786
01:01:57,246 --> 01:01:58,326
Finally.

787
01:01:58,846 --> 01:02:04,528
Not because you asked. Because we now own a building
with a monster in the basement, and

788
01:02:04,548 --> 01:02:09,777
the only security is a sixty-four-year-old man
with a flashlight and a paperback.

789
01:02:10,297 --> 01:02:12,715
Walter is a soldier.

790
01:02:13,235 --> 01:02:19,393
Walter is a man. That's the problem. Men can be
frightened. Men can be bribed. Men can

791
01:02:19,413 --> 01:02:22,697
be asked questions by the FBI
and answer them.

792
01:02:23,847 --> 01:02:29,940
Purchase confirmed. Security
specialist. Designation, Hale.

793
01:02:32,730 --> 01:02:36,021
Hale was waiting in the lobby when
the building opened.

794
01:02:36,592 --> 01:02:42,351
He was tall and broad through the shoulders, with a
shaved head and a black suit cut to

795
01:02:42,371 --> 01:02:47,998
hide the fact that he was built like a door. He had
a folder of credentials that said

796
01:02:48,018 --> 01:02:54,045
he had spent seventeen years in the Army and six in
private security, all of it verifiable,

797
01:02:54,065 --> 01:02:58,695
all of it perfect. He had a firm handshake
and a pleasant enough face.

798
01:02:59,264 --> 01:03:02,795
It was just that nobody could
remember seeing him blink.

799
01:03:03,485 --> 01:03:06,462
Mister — Hale, is it? Welcome. I'll
get you a badge.

800
01:03:06,983 --> 01:03:09,722
I have one, Mrs. Whitfield. Thank you.

801
01:03:10,242 --> 01:03:15,422
He did. It was clipped to his breast pocket. She had
not made it. It had her signature

802
01:03:15,443 --> 01:03:16,148
on the back.

803
01:03:16,717 --> 01:03:22,416
From that day on, Dana Whitfield called him Mister
Hale. She called Pell by his name,

804
01:03:22,436 --> 01:03:28,270
and Gus by his first name, and she had called Doctor
Lessing Howard for nineteen years.

805
01:03:28,291 --> 01:03:33,922
But the man in the black suit was always, without
her ever deciding it, Mister Hale.

806
01:03:35,492 --> 01:03:37,789
Walter Pruitt met him at shift change.

807
01:03:38,360 --> 01:03:44,119
The two of them stood in the security booth for a
few minutes, going over the rota and

808
01:03:44,139 --> 01:03:50,032
the cameras, two soldiers of very different vintage.
Walter was polite. Hale was polite.

809
01:03:50,052 --> 01:03:55,813
And the whole time, Walter watched the younger man's
eyes, the way he had once watched

810
01:03:55,833 --> 01:03:57,559
the eyes of men on patrol.

811
01:03:58,130 --> 01:04:04,282
He had known a lot of soldiers. Good ones and bad
ones, brave ones and broken ones. Every

812
01:04:04,302 --> 01:04:10,177
one of them, sooner or later, had looked at the
door. Checked the exit. Wondered, for

813
01:04:10,197 --> 01:04:12,050
a second, about going home.

814
01:04:12,619 --> 01:04:14,605
Hale never looked at the door.

815
01:04:15,295 --> 01:04:17,197
Where'd you serve, son?

816
01:04:17,715 --> 01:04:19,425
Wherever I was sent.

817
01:04:20,545 --> 01:04:26,438
Walter nodded slowly. Then he went home, and sat in
his kitchen with a cup of coffee he

818
01:04:26,458 --> 01:04:32,420
didn't drink, and thought, not for the first time
that month, that there was nobody home

819
01:04:32,440 --> 01:04:34,867
behind those eyes except the orders.

820
01:04:36,936 --> 01:04:38,833
The inspector came on Friday.

821
01:04:39,402 --> 01:04:45,094
Kuchar, before he had sold the building, had made
one phone call out of spite — to the

822
01:04:45,114 --> 01:04:51,074
state environmental office, to report what he
described as, quote, unauthorised industrial

823
01:04:51,094 --> 01:04:57,052
equipment and possible chemical hazards. It was the
most effective thing he had ever done,

824
01:04:57,072 --> 01:05:03,029
and he did not live to find that out, because he died
of a heart attack in a Florida condo

825
01:05:03,050 --> 01:05:07,414
nine years later, never having thought
about Division Seven again.

826
01:05:07,985 --> 01:05:14,076
The inspector's name was Ray Delgado. He was
forty-seven, and he had spent nineteen years

827
01:05:14,096 --> 01:05:20,327
walking around buildings that did not want him there,
being lied to by men in better shoes.

828
01:05:20,898 --> 01:05:23,322
Albert Wesker did not lie to him.

829
01:05:23,893 --> 01:05:29,744
That was the thing Delgado would remember
afterwards. The director met him at the door,

830
01:05:29,764 --> 01:05:35,751
walked him through every room himself, opened every
cabinet before he was asked, and took

831
01:05:35,770 --> 01:05:37,505
him to the basement first.

832
01:05:38,195 --> 01:05:40,277
That the unit? The one in the complaint?

833
01:05:40,797 --> 01:05:47,136
That's the unit. Arcturus. Industrial air handling for
the cold archive. Gus has the maintenance

834
01:05:47,157 --> 01:05:49,389
log. You're welcome to the manual.

835
01:05:49,909 --> 01:05:55,248
Delgado looked at the log. He looked at the anchor
bolts, and the service plate, and the

836
01:05:55,268 --> 01:06:00,545
permits on the wall, which were all there, and all
in order, and all — though he had no

837
01:06:00,565 --> 01:06:03,346
way of knowing this — less than
two weeks old.

838
01:06:04,635 --> 01:06:09,010
But you should see the solvent store on the
second floor. It's a disgrace.

839
01:06:09,929 --> 01:06:10,784
I'm sorry?

840
01:06:11,304 --> 01:06:17,277
The flammables cabinet in room two-oh-four is from the
nineteen-nineties. It isn't vented.

841
01:06:17,297 --> 01:06:23,003
The spill kit's expired. My predecessor ignored it
for years, because nobody ever made

842
01:06:23,023 --> 01:06:26,666
him fix it. I'd like you to make me
fix it. In writing.

843
01:06:27,987 --> 01:06:29,476
Delgado stared at him.

844
01:06:30,047 --> 01:06:34,166
In nineteen years, nobody had ever asked
him to write them up.

845
01:06:34,735 --> 01:06:40,751
The Cold-Read gave the Director the rest of the man
in a single glance: a decent public

846
01:06:40,770 --> 01:06:46,855
servant, underpaid and overworked, who had given his
career to a job most people treated

847
01:06:46,876 --> 01:06:53,029
as a nuisance, and who wanted — more than money, more
than promotion — simply to be taken

848
01:06:53,050 --> 01:06:53,722
seriously.

849
01:06:54,293 --> 01:06:59,007
So Albert Wesker took him seriously. It
cost nothing. It never does.

850
01:07:00,577 --> 01:07:07,259
Delgado's report was filed the following Wednesday.
It noted one deficiency — the solvent

851
01:07:07,278 --> 01:07:14,110
store, corrected within forty-eight hours — and
described the facility as, quote, exemplary

852
01:07:14,130 --> 01:07:20,661
in its cooperation. The complaint about unauthorised
industrial equipment was closed as

853
01:07:20,681 --> 01:07:21,414
unfounded.

854
01:07:21,983 --> 01:07:27,917
It would sit in a state database for three and a
half years, until someone from the FBI

855
01:07:27,938 --> 01:07:33,871
found it, and read it, and began to wonder why a
small research lab had gone out of its

856
01:07:33,891 --> 01:07:35,239
way to be inspected.

857
01:07:37,309 --> 01:07:40,221
Hannah Price found him through the
property records.

858
01:07:40,789 --> 01:07:46,719
She was thirty-one, a reporter at the Wilmington
Ledger, which had once had a newsroom

859
01:07:46,739 --> 01:07:52,737
of ninety and now had ten. She covered business,
courts, the city council, and anything

860
01:07:52,757 --> 01:07:58,894
else that happened when the other ten were busy. She
had been writing a short piece about

861
01:07:58,914 --> 01:08:04,913
the collapse of a British company called Umbrella
Holdings — local angle, jobs at risk,

862
01:08:04,932 --> 01:08:11,139
the usual — when she noticed that a building on the
south bank had just sold for two point

863
01:08:11,159 --> 01:08:14,873
nine million dollars to an LLC that
was nine days old.

864
01:08:15,443 --> 01:08:22,145
Then she noticed that the same LLC had just signed a
licence with a Chinese contract research

865
01:08:22,164 --> 01:08:28,286
company. Then she noticed that Verdant Axis — the
biggest biotech in the county — had

866
01:08:28,305 --> 01:08:34,427
quietly booked a one-point-two-million-dollar
charge in its latest filing for, quote,

867
01:08:34,448 --> 01:08:35,583
research access.

868
01:08:36,154 --> 01:08:41,423
Then she called the number on the LLC's
registration, and a woman named Dana said the

869
01:08:41,443 --> 01:08:43,851
Director would be happy to have coffee.

870
01:08:45,622 --> 01:08:51,030
They met at the same diner. He took the same booth.
He had, she noticed, already ordered

871
01:08:51,050 --> 01:08:56,088
for both of them, and what he had ordered for her
was what she would have ordered.

872
01:08:56,779 --> 01:08:58,306
You've read my stuff.

873
01:08:58,827 --> 01:09:03,974
All of it. Your piece on the port authority
contracts was very good. They should have

874
01:09:03,993 --> 01:09:05,676
let you run the second half.

875
01:09:06,197 --> 01:09:08,273
How do you know there was a second half?

876
01:09:08,792 --> 01:09:13,806
Because the first half ended on a question, and you
don't strike me as someone who asks

877
01:09:13,827 --> 01:09:15,427
a question you can't answer.

878
01:09:16,747 --> 01:09:21,416
She smiled despite herself. She would think
about that later, and be annoyed.

879
01:09:21,985 --> 01:09:27,475
The Cold-Read gave him Hannah Price in pieces.
Intelligence, first, bright and quick.

880
01:09:27,494 --> 01:09:33,309
Hunger — not for money, for the story that would get
her out of an fifteen-person newsroom

881
01:09:33,328 --> 01:09:38,948
before it became a six-person one. Suspicion, which
he liked. And something rarer, that

882
01:09:38,967 --> 01:09:44,457
took him a moment to name, because he had so seldom
seen it in his old world: she was

883
01:09:44,478 --> 01:09:45,623
not afraid of him.

884
01:09:46,193 --> 01:09:47,207
Not yet.

885
01:09:47,899 --> 01:09:53,070
So. Who are you, Doctor Wesker? Nine days ago, a dead
British company's lab in an industrial

886
01:09:53,091 --> 01:09:58,094
park. Today, a building, a licence in China, and
Verdant Axis's money. That's a very good

887
01:09:58,113 --> 01:09:58,658
nine days.

888
01:09:59,179 --> 01:10:02,594
It's a very good story. It's not the
best one in this town.

889
01:10:03,712 --> 01:10:04,881
I'm listening.

890
01:10:05,900 --> 01:10:11,271
Verdant Axis published the results of a Phase Two
trial in April. Their lead compound.

891
01:10:11,291 --> 01:10:16,599
The one that doubled their stock price. Look at the
dropout rate by trial site. Three

892
01:10:16,618 --> 01:10:22,051
of their nine sites lost almost no patients. The
other six lost a third of theirs. Then

893
01:10:22,072 --> 01:10:24,371
look at who ran the three good sites.

894
01:10:24,890 --> 01:10:26,186
And who ran them?

895
01:10:26,707 --> 01:10:30,332
That's your job, Miss Price. I'm just a
man with a spreadsheet.

896
01:10:31,851 --> 01:10:33,556
Why are you giving me this?

897
01:10:34,077 --> 01:10:38,872
Because Graham Tolliver tried to take my
building. I'd like him to be busy.

898
01:10:40,193 --> 01:10:45,896
She had expected him to lie. Everyone lied at this
stage — everyone had a noble reason,

899
01:10:45,916 --> 01:10:51,621
a public interest, a concern for the patients. He
simply told her the truth. It was the

900
01:10:51,640 --> 01:10:57,279
most disarming thing he could possibly have done,
and he knew it, and she knew he knew

901
01:10:57,300 --> 01:10:58,925
it, and it worked anyway.

902
01:10:59,614 --> 01:11:01,835
You know I'm going to look at you too.

903
01:11:02,354 --> 01:11:04,189
I'd be disappointed if you didn't.

904
01:11:05,909 --> 01:11:11,565
She left first. On the sidewalk she stopped, and
took out her notebook, and wrote three

905
01:11:11,586 --> 01:11:15,610
words under the name Umbrella. Then she
underlined them twice.

906
01:11:16,180 --> 01:11:17,228
Who is he.

907
01:11:18,399 --> 01:11:24,488
Across the street, in the shadow of a closed tyre
shop, a tall man in a black suit stood

908
01:11:24,509 --> 01:11:30,461
with his hands clasped in front of him, watching the
diner door. He had been there for

909
01:11:30,480 --> 01:11:34,350
an hour. He had not moved. Hannah
Price did not see him.

910
01:11:34,920 --> 01:11:39,805
It would be two years before she learned his name. By
then she would have learned a great

911
01:11:39,824 --> 01:11:42,395
many things, and would have
wished she had not.

912
01:11:45,216 --> 01:11:48,737
Assessment. One thousand one
hundred Points.

913
01:11:52,355 --> 01:11:55,969
The Director watched her go from a
window on the third floor.

914
01:11:56,658 --> 01:11:58,166
She will be a problem.

915
01:12:17,884 --> 01:12:20,604
Howard Lessing wrote the paper
in four nights.

916
01:12:21,174 --> 01:12:26,991
He wrote it at his kitchen table after his wife had
gone to bed, with a glass of Scotch

917
01:12:27,011 --> 01:12:32,693
he did not drink and the blue drive plugged into
the side of his laptop, and he wrote

918
01:12:32,713 --> 01:12:38,395
it better than anything he had written in twenty
years. It was twenty-two pages long.

919
01:12:38,415 --> 01:12:44,499
It had four figures. Its title was A Novel
Replication-Competent Viral Signature Associated

920
01:12:44,520 --> 01:12:48,524
With Extreme Proliferative Repair in
Human Peripheral Blood.

921
01:12:49,094 --> 01:12:51,124
It did not mention whose blood.

922
01:12:51,694 --> 01:12:57,609
He also wrote an email. It was shorter. It was
addressed to a vice-president of research

923
01:12:57,629 --> 01:13:03,408
at Verdant Axis Therapeutics, a man named Simon
Ferris who had once tried to hire him,

924
01:13:03,428 --> 01:13:09,612
and it said that Doctor Lessing had come into
possession of data of extraordinary scientific

925
01:13:09,632 --> 01:13:15,547
and commercial interest, and would welcome a
confidential conversation about his future.

926
01:13:16,116 --> 01:13:22,201
He told himself these were two separate things. The
paper was for science, and for history,

927
01:13:22,221 --> 01:13:28,104
and for the prize that had been promised to him at a
conference in nineteen ninety-four.

928
01:13:28,124 --> 01:13:30,116
The email was for his pension.

929
01:13:30,687 --> 01:13:36,491
He saved them both as drafts. He would send them on
Tuesday night, from the lab, after

930
01:13:36,511 --> 01:13:38,930
the preprint server's weekly update.

931
01:13:40,699 --> 01:13:45,966
On Monday morning, the Director held his weekly
meeting in the break room, and Howard

932
01:13:45,986 --> 01:13:51,315
Lessing sat at the far end of the table and tried to
look like a man who had spent the

933
01:13:51,335 --> 01:13:52,435
weekend gardening.

934
01:13:53,005 --> 01:13:58,765
It did not work. It would not have worked on anyone
paying attention. It certainly did

935
01:13:58,785 --> 01:14:04,681
not work on a man who could now read the human face
the way other people read subtitles.

936
01:14:05,250 --> 01:14:11,202
Guilt, in the crease beside his eye. Fear, in the
way he held his cup with both hands.

937
01:14:11,222 --> 01:14:17,242
And under them both, a thing the Cold-Read had no
single word for — a kind of trembling

938
01:14:17,262 --> 01:14:23,213
exultation, like a man standing on the edge of a
high diving board, about to jump, and

939
01:14:23,233 --> 01:14:25,365
sure the water would catch him.

940
01:14:25,935 --> 01:14:31,318
The Director said nothing. He talked about the
solvent store, and the Shenzhen licence,

941
01:14:31,338 --> 01:14:34,547
and the new coffee. He did not look
at Lessing once.

942
01:14:36,118 --> 01:14:39,436
At noon, Hale came to his office
and closed the door.

943
01:14:40,125 --> 01:14:45,717
Doctor Lessing has two drafts saved to the building's
mail server, Director. One is addressed

944
01:14:45,737 --> 01:14:51,027
to a preprint service. One to a Simon Ferris, at
Verdant Axis. He's also copied fourteen

945
01:14:51,047 --> 01:14:55,130
gigabytes from analyzer three to a
removable drive. I have the logs.

946
01:14:55,652 --> 01:14:56,804
Of course you do.

947
01:14:57,325 --> 01:14:59,211
Shall I take the drive?

948
01:14:59,730 --> 01:15:00,905
No. Let him bring it.

949
01:15:02,625 --> 01:15:07,736
The argument happened on the drive home that
night. It was the first real one.

950
01:15:08,427 --> 01:15:10,088
He is a traitor.

951
01:15:10,606 --> 01:15:13,399
He's a disappointed old man with
a thumb drive.

952
01:15:13,921 --> 01:15:20,251
He stole from you. He is going to sell you to your
enemy. In my time a man like that was

953
01:15:20,271 --> 01:15:25,591
not dismissed. He was made an example. So that
the next man would not try.

954
01:15:26,111 --> 01:15:31,315
In your time you had a secret police and a country
that didn't ask questions. Here, if

955
01:15:31,335 --> 01:15:36,478
Howard Lessing disappears, his wife calls the
Wilmington police on Wednesday morning,

956
01:15:36,498 --> 01:15:41,884
and the Wilmington police call his colleagues, and
one of his colleagues is Nadia Okafor,

957
01:15:41,904 --> 01:15:44,374
who is already measuring me for a coffin.

958
01:15:44,893 --> 01:15:47,625
Then do it so that nobody calls.

959
01:15:48,146 --> 01:15:53,111
Listen to me very carefully. We are not doing
that. Not now. Not for this.

960
01:15:54,431 --> 01:15:56,736
For a while there was only the road.

961
01:15:57,427 --> 01:15:58,559
Then what?

962
01:15:59,080 --> 01:16:04,908
A dead man is a problem. A frightened man is an
asset. I don't want Howard Lessing gone.

963
01:16:04,928 --> 01:16:05,972
I want him mine.

964
01:16:06,492 --> 01:16:11,080
A man who is afraid of you is not
yours. He is only afraid.

965
01:16:11,599 --> 01:16:15,605
That's what you never understood. For most
men, it's the same thing.

966
01:16:17,625 --> 01:16:23,496
On Tuesday night, at eight minutes to midnight,
Howard Lessing sat alone in lab two with

967
01:16:23,516 --> 01:16:27,579
the blue drive plugged in and both
drafts open on the screen.

968
01:16:28,147 --> 01:16:34,180
The building was empty. He had checked. Tomás had
left at six, Evelyn at ten — early,

969
01:16:34,200 --> 01:16:40,232
for her. Walter was off. The new security man,
Hale, was supposedly doing rounds, but

970
01:16:40,252 --> 01:16:45,928
Lessing had not seen him since eight, and had
decided that the new man was lazy.

971
01:16:46,498 --> 01:16:52,337
His finger was on the trackpad. The cursor was on the
button. He had imagined this moment

972
01:16:52,357 --> 01:16:57,999
for thirty years, and in all thirty years it had
looked like a stage in Stockholm, and

973
01:16:58,019 --> 01:17:04,057
not like a dim lab in an industrial park with a
flickering tube and the smell of old bleach.

974
01:17:04,625 --> 01:17:05,760
He took a breath.

975
01:17:07,450 --> 01:17:09,031
It's a good paper, Howard.

976
01:17:09,551 --> 01:17:14,041
Lessing made a sound that was not quite a
word, and spun the chair around.

977
01:17:14,611 --> 01:17:20,120
The Director was standing directly behind him. Close
enough to touch. Lessing had been

978
01:17:20,140 --> 01:17:25,715
facing the lab door, which was the only door, which
was forty feet away across an empty

979
01:17:25,735 --> 01:17:31,052
room, and which had not opened. He knew it had not
opened. He had been watching it.

980
01:17:31,741 --> 01:17:33,960
How — I didn't — how long have you —

981
01:17:34,479 --> 01:17:40,236
The figures are excellent. Figure three especially. The
division curve. You've found something

982
01:17:40,256 --> 01:17:45,768
nobody has ever found, and you've described it
clearly, and you've drawn exactly the right

983
01:17:45,788 --> 01:17:47,920
conclusion. It's a very good paper.

984
01:17:48,919 --> 01:17:50,137
It's also my blood.

985
01:17:52,157 --> 01:17:55,777
Lessing did not understand him at
first. Then he did.

986
01:17:56,347 --> 01:18:02,660
Control Seven. The calibration sample. The tube the
Director had handed Tomás with his

987
01:18:02,680 --> 01:18:03,322
own hand.

988
01:18:04,013 --> 01:18:09,327
That's — no. That's not possible. Those cells —
nothing human does that. Nothing —

989
01:18:09,847 --> 01:18:12,017
The Director took off his sunglasses.

990
01:18:13,587 --> 01:18:14,993
His eyes were red.

991
01:18:15,563 --> 01:18:21,639
Not bloodshot. Red, the way a coal is red. And the
pupils were not round. They were narrow

992
01:18:21,659 --> 01:18:27,736
and vertical, like a cat's, like a snake's, and they
widened, very slightly, as they found

993
01:18:27,756 --> 01:18:30,378
Howard Lessing's face in the lamplight.

994
01:18:30,947 --> 01:18:36,969
Lessing had been a scientist for thirty-four years.
He did not believe in monsters. And

995
01:18:36,989 --> 01:18:42,941
in that moment, looking up into those eyes from a
cheap office chair, some part of his

996
01:18:42,961 --> 01:18:48,914
mind older than science — older than language —
understood exactly what it was looking

997
01:18:48,934 --> 01:18:51,066
at, and screamed at him to run.

998
01:18:51,637 --> 01:18:54,933
He did not run. He could not make
his legs work.

999
01:18:57,123 --> 01:18:59,129
Nothing human does that. You're right.

1000
01:19:00,529 --> 01:19:05,010
So let me tell you what happens next, Howard,
and then you're going to choose.

1001
01:19:05,531 --> 01:19:11,167
He pulled a second chair around, and sat down
facing Lessing, knee to knee, the way a

1002
01:19:11,187 --> 01:19:13,097
doctor sits when he has news.

1003
01:19:13,786 --> 01:19:19,153
If you press that button, the preprint goes live
tomorrow at nine. It will be the most-read

1004
01:19:19,173 --> 01:19:24,185
paper in the world for about nineteen days. Then
someone will ask for the sample. And

1005
01:19:24,205 --> 01:19:29,335
there won't be one. The analyzer's memory was wiped
this afternoon — a firmware update,

1006
01:19:29,355 --> 01:19:34,722
very routine, Gus has the ticket. Tomás remembers
running a calibration that failed because

1007
01:19:34,742 --> 01:19:39,872
the reagents were bad. He's told Dana so, in
writing. There's no Control Seven. There's

1008
01:19:39,892 --> 01:19:45,023
no data. There's a fifty-eight-year-old scientist
who was passed over his whole career,

1009
01:19:45,043 --> 01:19:50,054
who stole files from his employer, emailed a
competitor for a job the same night, and

1010
01:19:50,074 --> 01:19:54,021
fabricated the most extraordinary result
in the history of biology.

1011
01:19:55,221 --> 01:20:01,007
Simon Ferris will forward your email to Verdant's
lawyers inside an hour. Your pension

1012
01:20:01,027 --> 01:20:06,746
goes. Your name goes. And every obituary they
eventually write about you will mention

1013
01:20:06,766 --> 01:20:08,569
it in the second paragraph.

1014
01:20:10,089 --> 01:20:11,629
And — the other choice?

1015
01:20:12,748 --> 01:20:18,053
You delete them. Both. Yourself, now, while I watch.
You give me the drive. And you stay.

1016
01:20:18,073 --> 01:20:23,199
You keep your job, and your salary goes up in
January, and one day — not soon, but one

1017
01:20:23,219 --> 01:20:27,506
day — you get to see what that blood becomes.
You get to be in the room.

1018
01:20:29,226 --> 01:20:34,686
Somewhere below them, very faint through the floor,
a compressor cycled. A long, slow

1019
01:20:34,706 --> 01:20:35,138
breath.

1020
01:20:35,707 --> 01:20:41,083
Lessing did not know what was in the basement. He
would not know for years. But he heard

1021
01:20:41,103 --> 01:20:46,478
the sound, and he saw the Director glance down toward
it, just once, with something that

1022
01:20:46,498 --> 01:20:51,689
looked almost like affection — and for the rest of
his life, when he could not sleep,

1023
01:20:51,709 --> 01:20:54,018
Howard Lessing would hear that breath.

1024
01:20:56,089 --> 01:20:57,875
He turned back to the laptop.

1025
01:20:58,445 --> 01:21:03,700
He deleted the paper. He deleted the email. He
emptied the trash folder without being

1026
01:21:03,720 --> 01:21:09,036
asked, and then he opened the mail server's drafts
folder and deleted it from there as

1027
01:21:09,056 --> 01:21:14,372
well, because he was a thorough man, and because he
wanted — he did not know why — for

1028
01:21:14,392 --> 01:21:18,094
the thing in front of him to see that
he was being thorough.

1029
01:21:18,665 --> 01:21:24,161
Then he pulled the blue drive out of the laptop and
held it out. His hand was shaking

1030
01:21:24,181 --> 01:21:27,861
so badly that the drive rattled
against his wedding ring.

1031
01:21:28,431 --> 01:21:34,358
The Director took it between his thumb and
forefinger, and squeezed. The casing split.

1032
01:21:34,378 --> 01:21:36,640
The chip inside folded like foil.

1033
01:21:37,331 --> 01:21:42,270
Thank you, Howard. Go home. Kiss your wife. Come in
at nine tomorrow. Don't be late.

1034
01:21:44,291 --> 01:21:49,677
Howard Lessing walked out to the parking lot, and
put one hand on the roof of his car,

1035
01:21:49,697 --> 01:21:51,375
and was sick on the tarmac.

1036
01:21:51,945 --> 01:21:57,623
Then he drove home, very carefully, under the speed
limit the whole way, and got into

1037
01:21:57,643 --> 01:22:02,786
bed beside his wife, and lay on his back in the
dark until the sky went grey.

1038
01:22:03,355 --> 01:22:07,462
He came in at nine. He was not late. He
was never late again.

1039
01:22:09,032 --> 01:22:15,157
He was never loyal, either. That is important, and it
is the thing the Director misjudged.

1040
01:22:15,177 --> 01:22:21,096
Howard Lessing would never love Umbrella, or believe
in it, or want it to win. He would

1041
01:22:21,116 --> 01:22:27,035
only be afraid of it. And fear, in a man with
nothing left to lose, has a half-life. It

1042
01:22:27,055 --> 01:22:29,220
decays. Slowly. Like everything.

1043
01:22:32,040 --> 01:22:34,488
Assessment. Traitor contained.

1044
01:22:38,594 --> 01:22:40,612
Level up. Level four.

1045
01:22:42,900 --> 01:22:46,043
Three into Intelligence. Two into Defence.

1046
01:22:46,733 --> 01:22:48,973
You have made a man who hates you.

1047
01:22:49,493 --> 01:22:54,315
I've made a man who obeys me. Hate's a hobby.
It doesn't show up to work.

1048
01:22:54,835 --> 01:22:58,954
In my experience, hate shows up to
everything eventually.

1049
01:22:59,474 --> 01:23:04,978
In your experience, you were surrounded by men who
hated you and you had them shot. And

1050
01:23:04,998 --> 01:23:06,249
then you still lost.

1051
01:23:07,768 --> 01:23:11,875
We shall see who is right about
Howard Lessing.

1052
01:23:12,397 --> 01:23:14,472
They would. It would take three years.

1053
01:23:16,541 --> 01:23:18,892
Evelyn Marsh noticed the next morning.

1054
01:23:19,463 --> 01:23:24,683
She noticed because she noticed everything about the
building now — she had started to

1055
01:23:24,703 --> 01:23:30,045
think of it, privately, as hers. Howard Lessing had
come in at nine exactly. He had said

1056
01:23:30,065 --> 01:23:35,224
good morning to everyone, including Tomás, whom he
had not said good morning to since

1057
01:23:35,244 --> 01:23:40,464
two thousand twenty-two. He was working on a routine
assay with the concentration of a

1058
01:23:40,484 --> 01:23:45,765
man defusing a bomb, and every few minutes, without
seeming to know he was doing it, he

1059
01:23:45,785 --> 01:23:46,983
looked at the floor.

1060
01:23:47,674 --> 01:23:48,781
Is Howard all right?

1061
01:23:49,301 --> 01:23:50,770
Howard's had a revelation.

1062
01:23:51,290 --> 01:23:52,608
What kind of revelation?

1063
01:23:53,128 --> 01:23:54,281
The religious kind.

1064
01:23:55,602 --> 01:24:01,347
She looked at him for a long moment. He looked back,
perfectly pleasant, sunglasses on,

1065
01:24:01,367 --> 01:24:02,806
and did not elaborate.

1066
01:24:03,374 --> 01:24:08,802
A more cautious person might have been frightened by
that answer. Evelyn Marsh was not

1067
01:24:08,822 --> 01:24:14,313
a cautious person. She went back to her bench
feeling something she would not have been

1068
01:24:14,333 --> 01:24:18,873
able to name, but which was, if she had been
honest, very close to envy.

1069
01:24:20,945 --> 01:24:27,256
Across town, in the eighteen-person newsroom of the
Wilmington Ledger, Hannah Price had

1070
01:24:27,276 --> 01:24:32,495
found the name of the man who ran Verdant
Axis's three good trial sites.

1071
01:24:33,065 --> 01:24:39,482
It was a private clinic network in Maryland. Its
registered owner was a limited partnership.

1072
01:24:39,502 --> 01:24:44,379
The limited partnership's general partner
was a man named Curtis Vane.

1073
01:24:44,949 --> 01:24:47,937
Curtis Vane was Graham Tolliver's
brother-in-law.

1074
01:24:48,507 --> 01:24:53,732
She sat back in her chair and looked at the board
for a long time. Then she picked up

1075
01:24:53,752 --> 01:24:58,854
her phone, and scrolled to a number she had saved
as Umbrella, and did not call it.

1076
01:24:59,425 --> 01:25:05,488
She was not a fool. She knew she was being used. She
had known it in the diner. The question

1077
01:25:05,508 --> 01:25:11,241
she could not stop asking herself — the question
that would drive the next two years of

1078
01:25:11,261 --> 01:25:14,879
her life — was not whether Albert
Wesker was using her.

1079
01:25:30,856 --> 01:25:34,671
In November, Tomás Reyes taught Albert
Wesker the internet.

1080
01:25:35,240 --> 01:25:41,306
It began by accident. The Director had come into the
break room at lunchtime for coffee,

1081
01:25:41,326 --> 01:25:47,185
and found Tomás scrolling, and asked him — in the
tone of a man asking about a rash —

1082
01:25:47,205 --> 01:25:48,776
what he was looking at.

1083
01:25:49,467 --> 01:25:54,453
It's just — it's a guy who restores old knives. He
finds them in rivers. Like, rusty.

1084
01:25:54,473 --> 01:25:55,807
And then he fixes them.

1085
01:25:56,328 --> 01:25:58,505
How many people are watching him fix them?

1086
01:25:59,025 --> 01:26:00,391
This one? Uh. Four million.

1087
01:26:01,511 --> 01:26:04,967
Four million people. Watching a
stranger clean a knife.

1088
01:26:05,489 --> 01:26:07,041
I mean, it's really satisfying.

1089
01:26:07,560 --> 01:26:09,123
The Director sat down.

1090
01:26:09,693 --> 01:26:15,765
He did not do that. In two months, nobody had seen
him sit in the break room. He sat down

1091
01:26:15,785 --> 01:26:19,668
across from Tomás, and took off his
sunglasses, and said:

1092
01:26:20,358 --> 01:26:21,425
Show me everything.

1093
01:26:22,946 --> 01:26:24,471
So Tomás showed him.

1094
01:26:25,041 --> 01:26:31,179
Short videos, ninety seconds long, served by a machine
that learned what you wanted before

1095
01:26:31,199 --> 01:26:37,064
you did. Feeds that never ended. Comment sections
where strangers who would never meet

1096
01:26:37,084 --> 01:26:43,017
decided, in their thousands, who was a hero and who
was a villain before lunch. Men who

1097
01:26:43,037 --> 01:26:48,970
had become famous for being angry. Women who had
become famous for being calm. Children

1098
01:26:48,990 --> 01:26:55,265
who had become famous for nothing at all. Numbers
everywhere — views and likes and followers

1099
01:26:55,285 --> 01:27:01,081
and shares — ticking upward like the odometer of a
car driven by the whole human race

1100
01:27:01,101 --> 01:27:01,629
at once.

1101
01:27:03,118 --> 01:27:04,016
Oh.

1102
01:27:04,535 --> 01:27:09,615
It was a very small word. Wesker had never
heard that voice make it before.

1103
01:27:10,305 --> 01:27:16,734
They have built it. They have built the machine. A
rally that never ends. A crowd in every

1104
01:27:16,754 --> 01:27:19,458
pocket. And no one is in charge of it.

1105
01:27:19,978 --> 01:27:24,289
Someone's always in charge of it. They just
don't stand on a balcony.

1106
01:27:24,808 --> 01:27:31,759
I spent a year of my life writing speeches for a
radio set in every kitchen. This — this

1107
01:27:31,779 --> 01:27:35,561
is a radio set in every hand,
that listens back.

1108
01:27:37,082 --> 01:27:39,243
You okay, Doc? You've gone kind of still.

1109
01:27:39,763 --> 01:27:41,492
How does it decide what to show them?

1110
01:27:42,013 --> 01:27:46,983
Honestly? Whatever makes them feel something. Angry
works best. Then scared. Then, like,

1111
01:27:47,003 --> 01:27:49,023
awe. Anything with awe does numbers.

1112
01:27:50,143 --> 01:27:52,569
The Director put his sunglasses back on.

1113
01:27:53,660 --> 01:27:55,090
You know you do that thing, right?

1114
01:27:55,609 --> 01:27:56,391
What thing?

1115
01:27:56,912 --> 01:28:01,269
The — okay, don't be mad. On the internet it's
called aura farming. When somebody does

1116
01:28:01,289 --> 01:28:05,747
something so effortlessly cool that everyone watching
kind of loses their mind a little.

1117
01:28:05,767 --> 01:28:10,072
Like, the way you just sat down and took the
glasses off and said show me everything.

1118
01:28:10,092 --> 01:28:14,244
That's aura. You're farming it. Constantly. It's
actually kind of insane to watch.

1119
01:28:14,764 --> 01:28:16,088
And aura can be farmed.

1120
01:28:16,608 --> 01:28:18,209
I mean — it's a joke. Kind of.

1121
01:28:18,730 --> 01:28:22,656
It is called charisma. We had it
before the telephone.

1122
01:28:23,177 --> 01:28:26,585
They've turned it into a number. How
very efficient of them.

1123
01:28:28,606 --> 01:28:32,959
The Shop had a blueprint that week that he had
been watching since October.

1124
01:28:34,280 --> 01:28:37,243
Blueprint. Rapid pathogen assay.

1125
01:28:44,975 --> 01:28:50,813
It was not a weapon. It was not a power. It was a
small plastic cartridge and a reader

1126
01:28:50,833 --> 01:28:56,944
the size of a paperback, that could tell a nurse in a
rural clinic what was making a child

1127
01:28:56,964 --> 01:29:02,257
sick in the time it took to make tea — and it
did not exist anywhere on Earth.

1128
01:29:02,947 --> 01:29:06,532
A medical device. You are buying
a thermometer.

1129
01:29:07,053 --> 01:29:13,323
I'm buying a face. Every empire needs one. Yours had a
uniform. Mine will have a hospital.

1130
01:29:14,474 --> 01:29:15,915
Purchase confirmed.

1131
01:29:20,263 --> 01:29:26,089
The documentation arrived the way Pell had arrived —
as if it had always been there. A

1132
01:29:26,109 --> 01:29:31,935
folder on the division's server, dated across
eighteen months, containing the complete

1133
01:29:31,955 --> 01:29:37,848
design history of a device that nobody in the
building had designed. Protocols. Reagent

1134
01:29:37,868 --> 01:29:43,830
lists. Failure analyses. Two rejected prototypes. Lab
notebooks, scanned, in handwriting

1135
01:29:43,850 --> 01:29:48,724
that matched, with unnerving precision, the
handwriting of Evelyn Marsh.

1136
01:29:50,794 --> 01:29:52,557
He gave it to her that evening.

1137
01:29:53,126 --> 01:29:59,264
She was at her bench, at seven, alone, running the
same sequencing job she had been running

1138
01:29:59,284 --> 01:30:05,082
for three weeks. He put a binder down beside her
keyboard, and when she opened it, and

1139
01:30:05,102 --> 01:30:08,871
saw her own handwriting on page one,
she went very pale.

1140
01:30:09,561 --> 01:30:11,333
This is — I didn't write this.

1141
01:30:11,854 --> 01:30:12,474
No.

1142
01:30:12,994 --> 01:30:18,858
It's my hand. It's my notation. I use that little
arrow for rate-limiting steps. Nobody

1143
01:30:18,878 --> 01:30:21,294
else does that. I didn't write this.

1144
01:30:21,814 --> 01:30:23,970
I know. It's still yours. If you want it.

1145
01:30:25,489 --> 01:30:30,959
She read for a while without speaking. She was a good
enough scientist to understand what

1146
01:30:30,979 --> 01:30:36,387
she was reading, and to understand that it worked,
and to understand that nothing in her

1147
01:30:36,407 --> 01:30:40,520
education could explain how it had come to
be sitting on her bench.

1148
01:30:41,090 --> 01:30:46,531
She could have asked. A more careful person would
have. Nadia Okafor would have asked

1149
01:30:46,551 --> 01:30:49,166
forty questions and then called a lawyer.

1150
01:30:49,734 --> 01:30:55,361
Evelyn Marsh closed the binder, and put both hands
flat on the cover, and looked up at

1151
01:30:55,381 --> 01:30:55,624
him.

1152
01:30:56,314 --> 01:30:57,292
You said not yet.

1153
01:30:57,812 --> 01:30:58,518
I did.

1154
01:30:59,037 --> 01:30:59,785
Is it now?

1155
01:31:01,107 --> 01:31:01,876
It's now.

1156
01:31:03,595 --> 01:31:08,827
It is worth recording what she felt, because it
mattered later. It was not gratitude.

1157
01:31:08,847 --> 01:31:14,265
Gratitude is something you feel toward an equal. It
was not quite love, though it looked

1158
01:31:14,285 --> 01:31:19,640
like it from the outside. It was the thing people
feel in cathedrals — the dizzy, total

1159
01:31:19,660 --> 01:31:25,016
surrender of a person who has spent her whole life
believing she was alone in the dark,

1160
01:31:25,036 --> 01:31:30,391
and has suddenly been shown that someone was
watching, and that the someone had a plan.

1161
01:31:30,961 --> 01:31:33,949
It was, in other words, the
beginning of a faith.

1162
01:31:36,020 --> 01:31:37,867
The speech happened on a Friday.

1163
01:31:38,436 --> 01:31:44,079
It was not planned as a speech. He had called
everyone into the break room at a quarter

1164
01:31:44,099 --> 01:31:49,611
to five to tell them about the assay — all of them,
the six who had been there before

1165
01:31:49,631 --> 01:31:55,143
him and the three who had arrived since. Dana,
Lessing, Evelyn, Tomás, Nadia, Walter,

1166
01:31:55,163 --> 01:32:00,805
who had come in early for it. Pell, standing
perfectly still by the fridge. Gus, by the

1167
01:32:00,825 --> 01:32:02,758
door. Hale, by the other door.

1168
01:32:03,328 --> 01:32:09,292
He had meant to talk for two minutes. He talked for
five. He did not use notes. Afterwards,

1169
01:32:09,312 --> 01:32:15,146
none of them could remember exactly what he had said,
only how it had felt, and when they

1170
01:32:15,166 --> 01:32:20,802
tried to explain it to their families, they sounded,
even to themselves, a little mad.

1171
01:32:21,492 --> 01:32:26,610
Two months ago, you were told something about
yourselves. You were told it by a letter

1172
01:32:26,630 --> 01:32:31,688
from London, and by a frozen bank account, and by
every empty chair in this building.

1173
01:32:31,708 --> 01:32:34,496
You were told that you were what
was left over.

1174
01:32:35,297 --> 01:32:41,531
The leftovers of a dead company. In a building
nobody visits. In a town nobody writes

1175
01:32:41,551 --> 01:32:44,622
songs about. Doing work that nobody reads.

1176
01:32:45,143 --> 01:32:46,321
He let that sit.

1177
01:32:47,011 --> 01:32:52,848
I'd like you to remember that feeling. Not because it
was true. Because every great thing

1178
01:32:52,868 --> 01:32:58,705
that has ever happened in this world began in a room
exactly like this one. A room nobody

1179
01:32:58,725 --> 01:33:02,061
was watching. With a few people
nobody had counted.

1180
01:33:02,961 --> 01:33:08,207
The men in glass buildings across that river don't
believe that. That's why they're going

1181
01:33:08,227 --> 01:33:09,035
to lose to us.

1182
01:33:10,355 --> 01:33:16,308
His voice had not risen. That was the uncanny thing.
It had not risen at all. But something

1183
01:33:16,328 --> 01:33:22,150
in the rhythm of it had changed — a cadence, a pulse,
a way of stepping from one sentence

1184
01:33:22,170 --> 01:33:27,795
to the next that made each one feel like a stair.
And behind his eyes, in the quiet of

1185
01:33:27,815 --> 01:33:33,636
the skull, the other half of him had leaned forward,
the way an old musician leans toward

1186
01:33:33,656 --> 01:33:36,852
an instrument he has not touched
in eighty years.

1187
01:33:37,542 --> 01:33:42,885
In twelve months, there will be a device in hospitals
that tells a doctor what is killing

1188
01:33:42,905 --> 01:33:48,308
a child in twelve minutes. It will have our name on
it. Not Verdant's. Not London's. Ours.

1189
01:33:48,328 --> 01:33:53,851
Umbrella. And every nurse who uses it will know that it
came from here — from this building,

1190
01:33:53,871 --> 01:33:55,357
from this room, from you.

1191
01:33:56,157 --> 01:34:01,444
That is the first thing. It is not the last thing. I
will not tell you tonight what the

1192
01:34:01,464 --> 01:34:06,811
last thing is, because you would not believe me. But
I will tell you this. I did not buy

1193
01:34:06,831 --> 01:34:08,641
this company to keep it alive.

1194
01:34:09,840 --> 01:34:11,633
I bought it to make it inevitable.

1195
01:34:13,654 --> 01:34:19,609
Tomás was the first on his feet. Then Dana. Then,
to his own surprise, Walter Pruitt,

1196
01:34:19,629 --> 01:34:25,795
who had sworn on the day he took off the uniform
never to stand up for an officer again.

1197
01:34:26,364 --> 01:34:32,193
Evelyn did not clap. She was crying, without seeming
to notice, her hands pressed together

1198
01:34:32,213 --> 01:34:33,622
in front of her mouth.

1199
01:34:34,192 --> 01:34:40,727
Pell, Gus, and Hale clapped in perfect unison, exactly
the same number of times, and stopped

1200
01:34:40,747 --> 01:34:45,500
at exactly the same moment. Nobody
noticed. They were all too busy.

1201
01:34:47,071 --> 01:34:49,323
Nadia Okafor did not clap.

1202
01:34:49,893 --> 01:34:55,504
She stood at the back with her arms folded, and she
watched the room. Not the man at the

1203
01:34:55,524 --> 01:35:01,071
front — the room. The faces. The way the applause
had come not from agreement, but from

1204
01:35:01,091 --> 01:35:06,510
something underneath agreement. She had seen that
look before, once, a long time ago,

1205
01:35:06,530 --> 01:35:12,077
at a church revival her grandmother had taken her to
in Lagos, when a preacher had made

1206
01:35:12,097 --> 01:35:15,725
a whole tent full of sensible people
fall to their knees.

1207
01:35:16,295 --> 01:35:21,925
It had frightened her then. It frightened her more
now, because this time there was no

1208
01:35:21,945 --> 01:35:27,642
God in the room. Only a man in a black shirt,
standing very still, with his hand on the

1209
01:35:27,662 --> 01:35:29,219
back of a plastic chair.

1210
01:35:32,038 --> 01:35:37,725
Assessment. Personnel morale, fervent.
Four thousand Points.

1211
01:35:39,946 --> 01:35:42,027
Level up. Level five.

1212
01:35:44,250 --> 01:35:50,086
Four thousand. For five minutes of talking in a break
room. More than the System had paid

1213
01:35:50,106 --> 01:35:55,810
him for the building, or the landlord, or the
traitor. More than everything else he had

1214
01:35:55,830 --> 01:35:57,916
done in two months put together.

1215
01:35:58,486 --> 01:36:04,269
The Wesker half of the mind noted the number, and
filed it, the way an investor files

1216
01:36:04,289 --> 01:36:10,072
a return. The Adolf half did not note it at all. It
was elsewhere. It was remembering

1217
01:36:10,092 --> 01:36:15,943
beer halls, and stadiums, and the roar of a crowd
coming back at it like surf — and it

1218
01:36:15,963 --> 01:36:19,971
was, for the first time in this new
life, completely happy.

1219
01:36:21,460 --> 01:36:22,827
Two of six.

1220
01:36:23,348 --> 01:36:24,203
Which two?

1221
01:36:24,721 --> 01:36:26,408
You know which two.

1222
01:36:28,558 --> 01:36:30,034
Command presence.

1223
01:36:35,428 --> 01:36:37,572
He bought it before he left the building.

1224
01:36:38,893 --> 01:36:40,335
Purchase confirmed.

1225
01:36:44,518 --> 01:36:50,144
At seven twenty the next morning, Tomás Reyes called
the Director's cell phone for the

1226
01:36:50,164 --> 01:36:51,654
first time in his life.

1227
01:36:52,344 --> 01:36:56,690
Okay, so, please don't kill me. I kind of filmed a bit
of it. Yesterday. Just, like, twenty

1228
01:36:56,710 --> 01:37:00,912
seconds. The bit about the room nobody was watching.
And I put it on my page, which has,

1229
01:37:00,932 --> 01:37:05,086
like, four hundred people, and I went to bed, and —
Doc, it's got forty thousand views.

1230
01:37:05,106 --> 01:37:08,157
It's got forty thousand views and people
are asking who you are.

1231
01:37:09,676 --> 01:37:11,974
The Director did not answer immediately.

1232
01:37:12,663 --> 01:37:16,654
Forty thousand. In one night.
From a break room.

1233
01:37:17,173 --> 01:37:18,518
Take it down, Tomás.

1234
01:37:19,039 --> 01:37:22,454
Yeah. Yeah, totally. I'm sorry, I'm —
taking it down now. I'm sorry.

1235
01:37:22,975 --> 01:37:25,941
Don't be sorry. It was good work.
It was just early.

1236
01:37:27,061 --> 01:37:27,643
Early?

1237
01:37:28,162 --> 01:37:29,700
Keep the file. We'll need it.

1238
01:37:31,420 --> 01:37:37,132
Tomás took it down. By then it had been downloaded
two hundred times, and three of those

1239
01:37:37,152 --> 01:37:42,865
copies would still be circulating on the internet
seven years later, in a very different

1240
01:37:42,885 --> 01:37:48,403
world, under titles like The First Speech, and
Where It Started, and Watch His Hands.

1241
01:37:49,973 --> 01:37:55,690
In lab two, at two in the morning, Evelyn Marsh was
building the first prototype of the

1242
01:37:55,710 --> 01:38:01,493
assay with her own hands, from a set of instructions
in her own handwriting that she had

1243
01:38:01,513 --> 01:38:02,417
never written.

1244
01:38:02,986 --> 01:38:08,835
She was humming as she worked. Four notes. Falling,
and falling, and falling, and landing

1245
01:38:08,855 --> 01:38:10,219
somewhere unresolved.

1246
01:38:26,689 --> 01:38:32,718
Graham Tolliver struck back in the first week of
December, and he struck the way frightened

1247
01:38:32,738 --> 01:38:37,970
rich men always strike: with lawyers, with
money, and with gossip, all at once.

1248
01:38:38,539 --> 01:38:44,756
The lawyers came first. A complaint filed in the
Delaware Court of Chancery, forty-one

1249
01:38:44,776 --> 01:38:51,356
pages long, alleging that Doctor Albert Wesker had
induced Verdant Axis to sign the archive

1250
01:38:51,376 --> 01:38:58,027
licence through fraudulent oral representations of
exclusivity. It was a weak case. Everyone

1251
01:38:58,047 --> 01:39:04,046
who read it knew it was a weak case. It was not
meant to win. It was meant to cost.

1252
01:39:04,618 --> 01:39:10,647
The money came second. On the same Tuesday, two
members of Umbrella's staff received job

1253
01:39:10,667 --> 01:39:16,558
offers from Verdant Axis. Tomás Reyes was offered a
senior technician's role at nearly

1254
01:39:16,578 --> 01:39:22,608
twice his salary. Evelyn Marsh was offered a position
as associate director of research,

1255
01:39:22,628 --> 01:39:28,863
with her own lab, her own budget, and a signing bonus
larger than everything she had earned

1256
01:39:28,883 --> 01:39:31,406
in the previous three years combined.

1257
01:39:31,977 --> 01:39:37,478
Evelyn forwarded hers to the Director within four
minutes of receiving it. She added one

1258
01:39:37,498 --> 01:39:38,356
word above it.

1259
01:39:38,927 --> 01:39:39,644
No.

1260
01:39:41,013 --> 01:39:43,025
Tomás did not forward his.

1261
01:39:44,594 --> 01:39:50,082
He sat in his car in the parking lot that evening for
forty minutes, looking at the offer,

1262
01:39:50,102 --> 01:39:55,284
and at the banking app that told him he still owed
a hundred and six thousand dollars

1263
01:39:55,304 --> 01:40:00,486
for a degree in biology he had been told would
change his life. He liked Umbrella. He

1264
01:40:00,506 --> 01:40:05,750
liked the Director, in the way you like a
thunderstorm. But a hundred and six thousand

1265
01:40:05,770 --> 01:40:10,033
dollars is a very specific number, and it
does not care what you like.

1266
01:40:10,603 --> 01:40:16,432
At nine o'clock there was a knock on his car window.
He jumped so hard he hit his head

1267
01:40:16,452 --> 01:40:17,249
on the roof.

1268
01:40:17,820 --> 01:40:23,526
The Director was standing in the rain, beside the
car, holding a folded sheet of paper.

1269
01:40:23,546 --> 01:40:28,989
He had no umbrella. He did not seem to be getting
wet in any way that bothered him.

1270
01:40:29,678 --> 01:40:31,417
Roll the window down, Tomás.

1271
01:40:31,938 --> 01:40:34,123
I — Doc, I was going to tell
you, I swear —

1272
01:40:34,642 --> 01:40:35,774
I know. Read this.

1273
01:40:36,295 --> 01:40:42,253
It was a letter. It gave Tomás Reyes a new title,
head of digital, which did not yet mean

1274
01:40:42,273 --> 01:40:48,232
anything. It gave him a salary exactly one dollar
higher than Verdant's. And it contained

1275
01:40:48,252 --> 01:40:53,942
a clause, drafted by Pell, under which Umbrella
would pay off the full balance of his

1276
01:40:53,962 --> 01:40:59,720
student loans over three years, provided he was
still employed at the end of each one.

1277
01:41:00,410 --> 01:41:02,224
This is — Doc. This is more than —

1278
01:41:02,744 --> 01:41:08,391
Verdant is offering you a job. I'm offering you a
job that doesn't exist yet. There's

1279
01:41:08,411 --> 01:41:11,057
a difference. One of them has a ceiling.

1280
01:41:12,377 --> 01:41:18,004
Tomás signed it on the steering wheel, in the dome
light, with a pen the Director handed

1281
01:41:18,024 --> 01:41:20,442
him. The ink ran a little in the rain.

1282
01:41:21,012 --> 01:41:26,858
It was, the Director would later calculate, the
cheapest loyalty he ever bought with money.

1283
01:41:26,878 --> 01:41:29,952
Every other kind he bought with
something worse.

1284
01:41:31,722 --> 01:41:37,864
The gossip came third, and it was the cleverest.
Someone at Verdant had noticed the Shenzhen

1285
01:41:37,884 --> 01:41:44,025
licence, and by the middle of December, a certain kind
of website had begun to ask a certain

1286
01:41:44,045 --> 01:41:49,918
kind of question: who was this Albert Wesker, with no
American history, who had appeared

1287
01:41:49,938 --> 01:41:54,806
from nowhere, and sold American biological
samples to a company in China?

1288
01:41:55,496 --> 01:42:01,023
That is good work. He is making you foreign. It
is the oldest trick there is.

1289
01:42:01,543 --> 01:42:02,291
You'd know.

1290
01:42:02,811 --> 01:42:07,143
I would. And I know how it ends when
you do nothing about it.

1291
01:42:07,664 --> 01:42:11,783
I'm not going to do nothing. I'm going to let
someone else do something.

1292
01:42:13,803 --> 01:42:17,589
Hannah Price's story ran on the
seventeenth of December.

1293
01:42:18,159 --> 01:42:23,748
It was the best piece of journalism the Wilmington
Ledger had published in a decade, and

1294
01:42:23,768 --> 01:42:29,167
she had checked every line of it three times,
because she knew who had pointed her at

1295
01:42:29,187 --> 01:42:32,928
it, and she was determined that
nothing in it would be his.

1296
01:42:33,496 --> 01:42:39,864
Nothing in it was. Every fact was her own. The three
trial sites with impossibly low dropout

1297
01:42:39,884 --> 01:42:45,974
rates. The Maryland clinic network that ran them. The
limited partnership that owned the

1298
01:42:45,994 --> 01:42:52,084
network. And the general partner of the limited
partnership: Curtis Vane, brother-in-law

1299
01:42:52,104 --> 01:42:58,124
of Graham Tolliver, who had been paid four point two
million dollars in trial fees over

1300
01:42:58,144 --> 01:43:04,095
two years, and who had, according to three former
nurses Hannah had found in two weeks

1301
01:43:04,115 --> 01:43:10,066
of phone calls, quietly removed patients from the
study who were not responding to the

1302
01:43:10,086 --> 01:43:10,414
drug.

1303
01:43:10,983 --> 01:43:17,034
By noon, the story had been picked up by two
national wire services. By one, the Food

1304
01:43:17,054 --> 01:43:23,462
and Drug Administration had announced that it was,
quote, reviewing the data. By the close

1305
01:43:23,482 --> 01:43:29,033
of trading, Verdant Axis Therapeutics had lost
sixty-one percent of its value.

1306
01:43:29,603 --> 01:43:35,569
Graham Tolliver watched it happen from his corner
office, on four screens, with his hand

1307
01:43:35,589 --> 01:43:42,166
over his mouth. At four o'clock his general counsel came
in and closed the door. At four-fifteen,

1308
01:43:42,186 --> 01:43:46,724
his wife called to ask why her brother was
not answering his phone.

1309
01:43:48,293 --> 01:43:54,526
Across the river, in a corner office with a water
stain shaped like Florida on the ceiling,

1310
01:43:54,546 --> 01:44:00,505
Albert Wesker watched the same line fall on a
nine-year-old monitor, and did not smile.

1311
01:44:01,075 --> 01:44:04,620
Smiling was for amateurs. He was
doing arithmetic.

1312
01:44:06,690 --> 01:44:12,549
The arithmetic was simple, and it was impossible. At
the close on the seventeenth, the

1313
01:44:12,569 --> 01:44:18,973
whole of Verdant Axis — its glass building, its hundred
and forty employees, its laboratories,

1314
01:44:18,993 --> 01:44:25,125
its drug pipeline, and ninety million dollars in cash
— could be bought on the open market

1315
01:44:25,145 --> 01:44:28,405
for about a hundred and seventy
million dollars.

1316
01:44:28,976 --> 01:44:30,633
Umbrella had two million.

1317
01:44:31,202 --> 01:44:36,490
He was still considering the problem at ten o'clock
that night when his phone rang, from

1318
01:44:36,510 --> 01:44:38,782
a number in Manhattan he did not know.

1319
01:44:39,471 --> 01:44:44,728
Doctor Wesker. My name is Catherine Adeyemi. I'm a
partner at Keel Harbour Capital. We

1320
01:44:44,748 --> 01:44:50,127
specialise in distressed life sciences. I won't keep
you long. I only have one question.

1321
01:44:50,647 --> 01:44:51,363
Go on.

1322
01:44:51,882 --> 01:44:53,719
You wrote that article, didn't you?

1323
01:44:55,438 --> 01:45:01,050
He did not answer immediately. It was the first time
in three months that a question had

1324
01:45:01,070 --> 01:45:02,969
arrived before he expected it.

1325
01:45:03,659 --> 01:45:06,435
Hannah Price wrote that article.
It's very good.

1326
01:45:06,954 --> 01:45:11,851
Hannah Price wrote it. You aimed it. Your company signs
a licence with Verdant in September.

1327
01:45:11,871 --> 01:45:16,502
Verdant tries to take your building in October.
Tolliver's lawyers sue you in December.

1328
01:45:16,522 --> 01:45:21,045
And three weeks later, a local reporter nobody's
heard of finds a four-million-dollar

1329
01:45:21,065 --> 01:45:25,642
family scheme buried in a clinic network in
Maryland. I've been doing this for fifteen

1330
01:45:25,662 --> 01:45:30,186
years, Doctor Wesker. Nothing in this business
happens three weeks later by accident.

1331
01:45:30,707 --> 01:45:31,667
And if I did?

1332
01:45:32,185 --> 01:45:36,617
Then I'd like to buy you a very expensive dinner and
discuss how you intend to pay for

1333
01:45:36,637 --> 01:45:38,842
the rest of the company you've
just broken.

1334
01:45:40,863 --> 01:45:45,652
She came to Wilmington herself, on the Monday,
on the eight o'clock train.

1335
01:45:46,221 --> 01:45:51,804
Catherine Adeyemi was thirty-eight, born in Lagos,
raised in South London, educated at

1336
01:45:51,824 --> 01:45:57,406
Cambridge and on the trading floors of the City, and
she had left a very senior job at

1337
01:45:57,426 --> 01:46:03,334
a very old bank after she had reported one of its
partners to the regulator, and discovered

1338
01:46:03,354 --> 01:46:09,066
what happens to people who do that. She had been at
Keel Harbour for four years. She was

1339
01:46:09,086 --> 01:46:14,212
very good at it. She was also, though she did
not tell people this, very bored.

1340
01:46:14,782 --> 01:46:20,491
She looked around the lobby, and at the water stain,
and at the nine-year-old monitors,

1341
01:46:20,511 --> 01:46:24,375
and at the man in black who rose to
meet her, and she said:

1342
01:46:25,065 --> 01:46:28,897
Well. This is either the worst investment I've
ever seen, or the best one.

1343
01:46:29,418 --> 01:46:30,401
Which do you think?

1344
01:46:30,921 --> 01:46:32,629
I think I'll know after lunch.

1345
01:46:34,149 --> 01:46:38,801
They talked for three hours. It was not the
kind of meeting he was used to.

1346
01:46:39,370 --> 01:46:44,713
The Command Presence did not work on her — or not
properly. He felt it reach for her,

1347
01:46:44,733 --> 01:46:50,327
the way a hand reaches for a railing in the dark, and
he felt it slide off. The Cold-Read

1348
01:46:50,347 --> 01:46:56,068
worked, but what it showed him was a woman reading him
back with nearly the same precision:

1349
01:46:56,088 --> 01:47:01,556
amusement, calculation, and something else,
flickering in and out, that took him a long

1350
01:47:01,576 --> 01:47:05,846
time to identify, because he had so rarely
seen it aimed at himself.

1351
01:47:06,414 --> 01:47:12,033
It was respect. And, beneath it, a small bright
thread of alarm, that she was choosing

1352
01:47:12,053 --> 01:47:14,655
— consciously, deliberately — to ignore.

1353
01:47:16,146 --> 01:47:21,274
Here's the structure. Keel Harbour lends Umbrella sixty
million on a bridge, secured against

1354
01:47:21,294 --> 01:47:26,310
your archive licences and the building, at a rate I'll
describe to my partners as punitive

1355
01:47:26,330 --> 01:47:31,290
and you'll describe to your staff as generous. You
buy just under five percent of Verdant

1356
01:47:31,310 --> 01:47:36,270
on the open market this week, quietly, below the
reporting line. Then you cross it, file,

1357
01:47:36,290 --> 01:47:41,082
and in January you launch a tender for control at a
premium to a price that's still on

1358
01:47:41,102 --> 01:47:45,838
the floor. The board will be desperate. Half of
them will be facing shareholder suits

1359
01:47:45,858 --> 01:47:49,363
over the trial data. You offer them a
clean exit. They take it.

1360
01:47:49,884 --> 01:47:50,739
And Tolliver?

1361
01:47:51,259 --> 01:47:56,813
Tolliver is a man whose brother-in-law is about to be
indicted. Tolliver is not your problem.

1362
01:47:56,833 --> 01:47:58,491
Tolliver is his own problem.

1363
01:47:59,812 --> 01:48:00,859
And what do you want?

1364
01:48:01,379 --> 01:48:06,409
Three things. If it closes, I want to be your chief
financial officer. I want equity.

1365
01:48:06,429 --> 01:48:10,212
And I want to know, at all times, where
the money actually goes.

1366
01:48:11,731 --> 01:48:13,686
He looked at her for a long moment.

1367
01:48:14,255 --> 01:48:19,942
The Adolf half of him did not like her at all. She
was clever, and she was not afraid,

1368
01:48:19,962 --> 01:48:25,715
and the old voice noticed — with the reflexes of the
poison it had once preached — that

1369
01:48:25,735 --> 01:48:31,820
she was not the kind of person it had ever allowed at
its table. Wesker noticed it noticing,

1370
01:48:31,840 --> 01:48:37,726
and found it contemptible. Not as a matter of
morality, which interested him very little.

1371
01:48:37,746 --> 01:48:43,366
As a matter of intelligence. And the Wesker half
understood what he was being offered

1372
01:48:43,386 --> 01:48:49,206
— a first-rate financial mind who could not be
summoned, could not be ordered, and could

1373
01:48:49,226 --> 01:48:53,586
therefore do the one thing none of his
loyal purchases ever could.

1374
01:48:54,154 --> 01:48:56,246
She could tell him when he was wrong.

1375
01:48:56,938 --> 01:48:57,510
Done.

1376
01:48:58,029 --> 01:48:59,236
Just like that?

1377
01:48:59,756 --> 01:49:03,534
I don't negotiate with people I've
already decided to hire.

1378
01:49:05,053 --> 01:49:10,946
She laughed — a short, surprised laugh — and shook
his hand. His grip was very cool. She

1379
01:49:10,966 --> 01:49:16,994
noticed that, too, and filed it with the other things
she was deciding not to think about.

1380
01:49:17,563 --> 01:49:21,871
He had agreed to all three of her conditions.
He would honour two of them.

1381
01:49:23,942 --> 01:49:29,870
Graham Tolliver's last move was the stupidest one, and
he made it the night after Catherine

1382
01:49:29,890 --> 01:49:30,915
Adeyemi's visit.

1383
01:49:31,486 --> 01:49:37,284
He had heard, from Dale Kuchar, a rambling story
about a machine in a basement that had

1384
01:49:37,304 --> 01:49:43,035
not been there before. He had decided — in the way
that desperate men decide things at

1385
01:49:43,055 --> 01:49:48,986
two in the morning — that if he could prove Umbrella
was running an unlicensed biological

1386
01:49:49,006 --> 01:49:54,871
facility, he could take the whole thing to the state
and bury it. So he paid two retired

1387
01:49:54,891 --> 01:50:00,756
police officers who did private work, and they went
to the building, and they forced the

1388
01:50:00,776 --> 01:50:04,903
basement fire door with a pry bar at
two-forty in the morning.

1389
01:50:05,471 --> 01:50:10,841
They had flashlights and a camera. They found the
freezers. They found the grey unit in

1390
01:50:10,861 --> 01:50:12,204
the corner, breathing.

1391
01:50:12,774 --> 01:50:14,837
One of them lifted the camera.

1392
01:50:16,407 --> 01:50:18,269
Hale was standing in front of him.

1393
01:50:18,837 --> 01:50:24,522
Neither man had heard him arrive. Neither man had
heard Gus, either, who was standing

1394
01:50:24,542 --> 01:50:30,494
two feet to Hale's left, holding a mop, with an
expression of mild professional interest.

1395
01:50:30,514 --> 01:50:32,104
Neither of them blinked.

1396
01:50:32,794 --> 01:50:34,726
Good morning, gentlemen.

1397
01:50:36,446 --> 01:50:42,140
What happened in the next four minutes, the two men
never told anyone. Nobody was hurt.

1398
01:50:42,160 --> 01:50:47,854
The Director had been very clear about that, and
Hale did not do things he had not been

1399
01:50:47,874 --> 01:50:53,569
told to do. There was no violence. There were only
four minutes in a dark basement with

1400
01:50:53,589 --> 01:50:59,283
two men who did not blink, and a machine that
breathed, and — one of the men would say,

1401
01:50:59,303 --> 01:51:04,998
years later, drunk, to a bartender in Arizona who
did not believe him — a small frosted

1402
01:51:05,018 --> 01:51:10,910
window in the side of the machine, through which, for
a moment, something had looked back.

1403
01:51:12,480 --> 01:51:18,334
They left without the camera. Both of them resigned
from private work the next morning.

1404
01:51:18,354 --> 01:51:21,576
One moved to Arizona. The other
joined a church.

1405
01:51:22,145 --> 01:51:25,432
Neither of them ever called Graham
Tolliver again.

1406
01:51:28,253 --> 01:51:31,988
Assessment. Five thousand six
hundred Points.

1407
01:51:38,678 --> 01:51:44,818
On Christmas Eve, Umbrella filed with the Securities
and Exchange Commission that it had

1408
01:51:44,838 --> 01:51:51,189
acquired five point two percent of Verdant Axis
Therapeutics, and intended to seek control.

1409
01:51:51,759 --> 01:51:57,525
On Christmas Day, Graham Tolliver read the filing at
his dining table, in front of his

1410
01:51:57,545 --> 01:52:03,311
wife, and his children, and his brother-in-law's
empty chair, and understood, at last,

1411
01:52:18,862 --> 01:52:24,794
The tender offer went out on the fourth of January,
at four dollars and ten cents a share

1412
01:52:24,814 --> 01:52:30,679
— thirty-four percent above the price Verdant Axis
had closed at on the last trading day

1413
01:52:30,699 --> 01:52:36,230
of the year, and fifty percent below where it had
been before Hannah Price's story.

1414
01:52:36,799 --> 01:52:43,258
It was, the financial press agreed, an opportunistic
offer. It was also, the financial

1415
01:52:43,278 --> 01:52:46,422
press agreed, very probably going to work.

1416
01:52:46,992 --> 01:52:53,074
On the ninth of January, Curtis Vane was indicted in
federal court in Baltimore on fourteen

1417
01:52:53,094 --> 01:52:59,245
counts of healthcare fraud. On the tenth, three of
Verdant's nine directors received letters

1418
01:52:59,265 --> 01:53:05,280
from shareholder lawyers. On the twelfth, the
company's auditors announced that they would

1419
01:53:05,300 --> 01:53:09,372
be, quote, unable to sign the annual
accounts pending review.

1420
01:53:09,941 --> 01:53:15,639
By the fifteenth, holders of thirty-eight percent of
the company had tendered their shares

1421
01:53:15,659 --> 01:53:21,039
to Umbrella. The board called a special meeting for
the nineteenth, to decide whether

1422
01:53:21,059 --> 01:53:23,581
to recommend the offer to everyone else.

1423
01:53:24,150 --> 01:53:29,504
Catherine Adeyemi spent the week before the meeting
on the phone. She spoke to all nine

1424
01:53:29,524 --> 01:53:34,815
directors, separately. She did not threaten any of
them. She simply explained, to each

1425
01:53:34,835 --> 01:53:40,127
one, in the calm voice of a woman who had already
done the maths on their behalf, what

1426
01:53:40,147 --> 01:53:45,562
the next two years of their lives would look like if
the offer failed — the depositions,

1427
01:53:45,582 --> 01:53:51,120
the insurance claims, the headlines — and what they
would look like if it succeeded, which

1428
01:53:51,140 --> 01:53:51,799
was: quiet.

1429
01:53:52,370 --> 01:53:57,428
By the eighteenth, eight of the nine had told her,
off the record, which way they would

1430
01:53:57,448 --> 01:53:57,719
vote.

1431
01:53:58,290 --> 01:54:00,234
The ninth was Graham Tolliver.

1432
01:54:02,303 --> 01:54:08,081
The Verdant Axis boardroom was on the twelfth
floor, behind glass, with a view of the

1433
01:54:08,101 --> 01:54:14,220
river and, on the far bank, if you knew where to look,
a small brick building with a faded

1434
01:54:14,240 --> 01:54:14,561
sign.

1435
01:54:15,132 --> 01:54:21,035
The meeting was called for ten. At ten, eight
directors, two lawyers, Catherine Adeyemi,

1436
01:54:21,055 --> 01:54:27,025
and Mister Pell were seated at the long table. Graham
Tolliver was standing at the window

1437
01:54:27,045 --> 01:54:28,775
with his back to the room.

1438
01:54:29,345 --> 01:54:31,280
Albert Wesker was not there.

1439
01:54:31,851 --> 01:54:36,067
At ten past ten, Tolliver turned
around and began to speak.

1440
01:54:37,257 --> 01:54:40,886
I'll say what everyone's thinking. He's late. The man
who wants to own this company can't

1441
01:54:40,906 --> 01:54:44,412
be bothered to show up for the meeting where we
decide whether to sell it to him. That

1442
01:54:44,432 --> 01:54:47,897
tells you everything. He's a hustler. He came out
of nowhere, he fed a hit piece to a

1443
01:54:47,917 --> 01:54:51,423
local rag, he's got a licence with a company in
Shenzhen, and he's got a basement full

1444
01:54:51,443 --> 01:54:55,236
of — nobody even knows what. And you want to hand him a
hundred and forty jobs and a pipeline

1445
01:54:55,256 --> 01:54:56,629
that took me eight years to build?

1446
01:54:57,549 --> 01:55:00,241
Nobody answered him. So he kept talking.

1447
01:55:00,811 --> 01:55:06,747
This is the great secret of the powerful, and it is
not complicated. When a room is silent,

1448
01:55:06,767 --> 01:55:12,441
the frightened man fills it. He fills it with
arguments, and then with accusations, and

1449
01:55:12,461 --> 01:55:18,003
then — because the silence is still there, and he
cannot bear it — with things he did

1450
01:55:18,023 --> 01:55:19,050
not mean to say.

1451
01:55:19,740 --> 01:55:24,219
That data was clean. The trial was clean. Curtis
made some mistakes with the paperwork,

1452
01:55:24,239 --> 01:55:28,821
fine, but the drug works. It works. And if anyone at
this table had had the guts to stand

1453
01:55:28,841 --> 01:55:31,923
behind it instead of reading the
Wilmington goddamn Ledger —

1454
01:55:32,624 --> 01:55:36,486
— and I'll tell you something else. I knew about the
sites. Okay? I knew the numbers looked

1455
01:55:36,506 --> 01:55:40,113
good. Everyone knew. That's what you do. That's the
business. Everybody at this table

1456
01:55:40,133 --> 01:55:40,368
knew —

1457
01:55:42,089 --> 01:55:43,126
He stopped.

1458
01:55:43,696 --> 01:55:49,136
One of the lawyers had very slowly put down his pen.
Two of the directors were looking

1459
01:55:49,156 --> 01:55:54,596
at each other. Catherine Adeyemi, at the far end of
the table, had not moved a muscle,

1460
01:55:54,616 --> 01:55:59,992
but had — the lawyer next to her would say later —
the expression of a woman watching

1461
01:56:00,012 --> 01:56:03,927
someone walk into a glass door she had
spent a week polishing.

1462
01:56:05,498 --> 01:56:06,704
The door opened.

1463
01:56:07,274 --> 01:56:13,084
Albert Wesker walked in. He wore a black coat, and
black gloves, and his sunglasses, and

1464
01:56:13,104 --> 01:56:18,715
he did not apologise for being late, and he did not
take a seat. He walked the length

1465
01:56:18,735 --> 01:56:24,479
of the room, without hurrying, while nine people
watched him, and stopped at the end of

1466
01:56:24,499 --> 01:56:27,393
the table directly opposite
Graham Tolliver.

1467
01:56:27,964 --> 01:56:33,038
He had been standing outside the glass door for
twelve minutes. He had heard everything.

1468
01:56:35,108 --> 01:56:41,188
He did not speak for a while. The silence came back,
heavier than before. Tolliver opened

1469
01:56:41,208 --> 01:56:43,313
