3715
05:02:31,270 --> 05:02:32,936
Wesker did not answer that.

3716
05:02:33,506 --> 05:02:38,931
He looked at the screen, at the grey figure waiting
in the smoke. And he found — with

3717
05:02:38,951 --> 05:02:44,632
the precise, clinical honesty that was the best and
worst thing about him — that he could

3718
05:02:44,652 --> 05:02:47,001
not say that the old voice was wrong.

3719
05:02:49,572 --> 05:02:55,344
At a quarter past nine that morning, in a windowless
room at Langley, an analyst showed

3720
05:02:55,364 --> 05:03:01,402
Martin Sloane a sequence of thermal images from a
satellite that had passed over the Mojave

3721
05:03:01,422 --> 05:03:03,332
Desert at two in the morning.

3722
05:03:03,903 --> 05:03:10,593
Twelve small heat signatures, entering a compound. One
very large heat signature, emerging

3723
05:03:10,613 --> 05:03:17,228
from somewhere beneath it. The twelve small
signatures, one by one, fading. A helicopter,

3724
05:03:17,248 --> 05:03:18,346
leaving, alone.

3725
05:03:18,917 --> 05:03:24,507
Sloane watched the sequence four times. Then he
straightened up, and put his hands in

3726
05:03:24,527 --> 05:03:30,448
his pockets, and said, to nobody in particular, in a
