3809
05:10:50,974 --> 05:10:56,902
The Adolf half of the mind had been silent since the
car had turned into Whitehall. It

3810
05:10:56,922 --> 05:11:01,398
was silent for a long time. When it
spoke, its voice was strange.

3811
05:11:02,087 --> 05:11:04,308
I tried to burn this city.

3812
05:11:04,828 --> 05:11:05,512
I know.

3813
05:11:06,032 --> 05:11:11,956
Night after night. Fifty-seven nights in a row, in
the first autumn. I stood in front

3814
05:11:11,976 --> 05:11:18,041
of maps with pins in them and I chose streets. I
wanted them to break. I wanted them to

3815
05:11:18,061 --> 05:11:18,320
beg.

3816
05:11:18,840 --> 05:11:19,657
They didn't.

3817
05:11:20,178 --> 05:11:21,822
No. They did not.

3818
05:11:23,542 --> 05:11:28,682
And now their Prime Minister just shook your hand,
and thanked you for the jobs, and a

3819
05:11:28,702 --> 05:11:30,242
waiter gave you champagne.

3820
05:11:31,761 --> 05:11:33,960
Do you think that is funny?

3821
05:11:34,479 --> 05:11:39,674
I think it's the whole lesson. You came at them with
bombers, and they fought you in the

3822
05:11:39,694 --> 05:11:43,645
streets. I came with two billion pounds,
and they held a reception.

3823
05:11:45,664 --> 05:11:52,348
The Wesker half of the mind meant it as a cruelty. He
usually did. But the old voice did

3824
05:11:52,368 --> 05:11:57,452
not respond the way he expected. It did
not rage. It did not argue.

3825
05:11:58,022 --> 05:12:03,946
It simply looked out of the window at the rain on the
parade ground, through eyes it shared,

3826
05:12:03,966 --> 05:12:09,632
for a very long time, and said nothing at all. And
the Wesker half of the mind could not

3827
05:12:09,652 --> 05:12:13,897
tell — for the first time since the bunker
— what it was thinking.

3828
05:12:16,467 --> 05:12:22,408
Catherine Adeyemi had come with him. She was talking
to the chief executive of a British

3829
05:12:22,428 --> 05:12:28,369
bank about bond markets, and hating it, when a
patrician man in a dinner jacket appeared

3830
05:12:28,389 --> 05:12:33,044
at her elbow with two fresh glasses of
champagne, and handed her one.

3831
05:12:33,734 --> 05:12:39,623
Miss Adeyemi. Julian Ashcombe. We've never met. I'm
with the Foreign Office. Well — adjacent

3832
05:12:39,643 --> 05:12:40,009
to it.

3833
05:12:40,529 --> 05:12:41,608
Adjacent.

3834
05:12:42,128 --> 05:12:47,084
Very adjacent. I read about you, some years ago. The
business at Ashworth Grey. The partner,

3835
05:12:47,104 --> 05:12:51,735
and the regulator, and what happened to you
afterwards. I thought it was very brave. I

3836
05:12:51,755 --> 05:12:55,143
thought it was also very badly handled,
by everyone except you.

3837
05:12:56,662 --> 05:13:02,208
She looked at him for a long moment. Nobody had
mentioned Ashworth Grey to her in six

3838
05:13:02,228 --> 05:13:04,238
years. She had made sure of it.

3839
05:13:04,928 --> 05:13:06,785
What do you want, Mister Ashcombe?

3840
05:13:07,304 --> 05:13:12,177
Oh, nothing tonight. Tonight I only wanted to
say hello. And to give you this.

3841
05:13:12,698 --> 05:13:18,016
It was a plain white card, with a London telephone
number on it, and nothing else.

3842
05:13:18,707 --> 05:13:23,538
You once asked a very large institution a very simple
question, Miss Adeyemi. Where does

3843
05:13:23,558 --> 05:13:28,445
the money go. I've always admired people who ask that
question. If you ever find yourself

3844
05:13:28,465 --> 05:13:33,352
asking it again — about anyone — you might ring that
