a data centre in Oregon, and a single goal: to
build, for Sebastian Crane, what Oracle

4703
06:26:40,218 --> 06:26:46,556
believed Albert Wesker had. An artificial optimiser
that would watch Crane's every decision,

4704
06:26:46,576 --> 06:26:52,845
reward the good ones, and — somehow, from the sum of
all human knowledge in Lumen's servers

4705
06:26:52,865 --> 06:26:55,955
— hand him capabilities that
nobody else had.

4706
06:26:56,525 --> 06:27:02,426
It did not work. It could not work. It produced very
good advice, and several patents,

4707
06:27:02,446 --> 06:27:08,622
and a great deal of analysis. But it did not produce a
single orphan idea — a single thing

4708
06:27:08,642 --> 06:27:14,474
that had not been, somewhere, in the data it had
learned from. Everything it gave him

4709
06:27:14,494 --> 06:27:15,300
had parents.

4710
06:27:15,870 --> 06:27:21,585
The thing that Albert Wesker had did not come from
the data. It came from outside. And

4711
06:27:21,605 --> 06:27:27,387
Sebastian Crane, who did not believe in anything
that could not be modelled, spent four

4712
06:27:27,407 --> 06:27:33,122
months and nine billion dollars proving to himself
that it could not be modelled — and

4713
06:27:33,142 --> 06:27:35,990
then refused to believe what
he had proved.

4714
06:27:38,059 --> 06:27:43,982
They met in June, on a rooftop in San Francisco,
after a conference at which they had

4715
06:27:44,002 --> 06:27:47,758
both been scheduled to speak and
only one of them had.

4716
06:27:48,328 --> 06:27:53,754
Crane walked straight up to him at the railing,
without introduction, and without small

4717
06:27:53,774 --> 06:27:56,071
talk, which the Director appreciated.

4718
06:27:56,761 --> 06:27:58,064
What's feeding you?

4719
06:27:58,583 --> 06:27:59,438
I'm sorry?

4720
06:27:59,958 --> 06:28:04,408
I've modelled you. I've put everything you've ever
done into the largest predictive system

4721
06:28:04,428 --> 06:28:08,680
on Earth, and it can predict everyone except you. It
says your decisions are optimised

4722
06:28:08,700 --> 06:28:13,100
against an external reward function. It says
sixty-three of your patents have no parents.

4723
06:28:13,120 --> 06:28:16,429
It says something is giving you answers. I
want to know what it is.

4724
06:28:18,448 --> 06:28:24,807
The Director looked at him for a long time. The
Cold-Read gave him Sebastian Crane in

4725
06:28:24,827 --> 06:28:31,712
a single glance, and it was an almost comically simple
face to read. Intelligence, enormous.

4726
06:28:31,732 --> 06:28:38,090
Curiosity, enormous. Ego, larger than both. And
envy — raw, childish, total envy — of

4727
06:28:38,110 --> 06:28:41,617
the kind a boy feels for another
boy's bicycle.

4728
06:28:42,307 --> 06:28:45,210
Ambition, Mister Crane. It's what
feeds all of us.

4729
06:28:45,730 --> 06:28:50,150
Don't. I know it isn't. Oracle knows it isn't. I've
spent nine billion dollars trying

4730
06:28:50,170 --> 06:28:53,283
to build what you have, and I can't,
and I need to know why.

4731
06:28:53,804 --> 06:28:56,850
Then you've already learned the most
important thing about it.

4732
06:28:57,369 --> 06:28:58,224
Which is?

4733
06:28:58,744 --> 06:28:59,673
That it's mine.

4734
06:29:02,193 --> 06:29:08,165
He finished his drink, and set down the glass on the
railing, and walked away. And Sebastian

4735
06:29:08,185 --> 06:29:14,158
Crane stood on the rooftop for a long time, looking at
the lights of the bridge, and decided

4736
06:29:14,178 --> 06:29:19,695
— as men like him always decide, when they are told
something is not theirs — to take

4737
06:29:19,715 --> 06:29:19,891
it.

4738
06:29:22,710 --> 06:29:26,652
Ability upgrade. Thought acceleration two.

4739
06:29:28,905 --> 06:29:34,585
He bought it on the flight home. Six hundred to one.
A single second became ten minutes.

4740
06:29:34,605 --> 06:29:40,479
And, stranger still, he could run three of them at
once — three separate trains of thought,

4741
06:29:40,499 --> 06:29:46,244
in three separate rooms of the Memory Palace, each
with its own ten minutes, each unaware

4742
06:29:46,264 --> 06:29:49,612
of the others until he chose to
bring them together.

4743
06:29:50,181 --> 06:29:52,722
He used all three on Sebastian Crane.

4744
06:29:54,912 --> 06:29:59,052
Why not simply buy him? Or break him,
like the policeman?

4745
06:29:59,571 --> 06:30:04,836
Because he has a machine that can see the shape of
what we are. Buying him won't turn

4746
06:30:04,856 --> 06:30:10,617
off the machine. Breaking him won't turn it off. The
only way to beat a machine that predicts

4747
06:30:10,637 --> 06:30:13,103
you is to teach it the wrong prediction.

4748
06:30:13,624 --> 06:30:15,798
You intend to lie to it.

4749
06:30:16,318 --> 06:30:22,251
I intend to be honest with it. For six months.
Perfectly, predictably honest. And then,

4750
06:30:22,271 --> 06:30:24,852
once, at the moment that matters, not.

4751
06:30:27,371 --> 06:30:31,843
For six months, the Director made decisions
that Oracle could predict.

4752
06:30:32,412 --> 06:30:38,265
He did it deliberately. Every time he acted, he acted
in a way that fit the pattern Oracle

4753
06:30:38,285 --> 06:30:44,138
had drawn of him — the external reward, the optimiser,
the leap. And Oracle, which learned

4754
06:30:44,158 --> 06:30:49,815
from everything, learned from this. Its predictions
of Umbrella improved. Sixty percent

4755
06:30:49,835 --> 06:30:55,688
accuracy, then seventy, then eighty-five. By December,
Oracle was predicting Albert Wesker

4756
06:30:55,708 --> 06:31:01,430
with ninety-one percent confidence, and Sebastian
Crane was sleeping four hours a night,

4757
06:31:01,450 --> 06:31:02,408
and very happy.

4758
06:31:02,979 --> 06:31:08,912
And in December, Oracle made its biggest prediction
of all. It said, with ninety-seven

4759
06:31:08,932 --> 06:31:15,557
percent confidence, that Umbrella was about to launch a
bid for the world's largest manufacturer

4760
06:31:15,577 --> 06:31:21,718
of the specialised chips used to train artificial
intelligence — a company in Taiwan that

4761
06:31:21,738 --> 06:31:27,741
Lumen depended on for everything. The pattern was
unmistakable. Every move the Director

4762
06:31:27,761 --> 06:31:30,372
had made for six months pointed at it.

4763
06:31:30,943 --> 06:31:32,542
So Crane moved first.

4764
06:31:33,111 --> 06:31:40,022
On the ninth of January, twenty thirty-four, Lumen
announced a two-hundred-and-forty-billion-dollar

4765
06:31:40,042 --> 06:31:46,253
offer for the Taiwanese chip manufacturer. It was the
largest technology deal in history.

4766
06:31:46,273 --> 06:31:52,204
It committed every dollar Lumen had and a great
many it did not. It was designed — as

4767
06:31:52,224 --> 06:31:57,456
Crane told his board, with shining eyes — to
get there before Umbrella did.

4768
06:31:58,025 --> 06:31:59,534
Umbrella never bid.

4769
06:32:02,103 --> 06:32:08,058
Umbrella had never been going to bid. Umbrella had
spent six months making every public

4770
06:32:08,078 --> 06:32:14,032
move that a company about to bid would make, and had
not made a single private one. And

4771
06:32:14,052 --> 06:32:19,869
in the week after Lumen's announcement, when the
governments of Taiwan and the United

4772
06:32:19,889 --> 06:32:25,706
States and China all realised at the same moment
that the company that ran a third of

4773
06:32:25,726 --> 06:32:31,749
the world's cloud was about to own the chips that ran
the rest, the deal became the most

4774
06:32:31,769 --> 06:32:34,840
politically radioactive
transaction on Earth.

4775
06:32:35,410 --> 06:32:41,553
It was blocked in March. Lumen had spent twenty
billion dollars on advisors, committed

4776
06:32:41,573 --> 06:32:47,717
its balance sheet, and lost the confidence of every
regulator on three continents. Its

4777
06:32:47,737 --> 06:32:53,881
stock fell forty-four percent. Its board removed
Sebastian Crane as chief executive on

4778
06:32:53,901 --> 06:32:55,314
the second of April.

4779
06:32:55,886 --> 06:33:02,170
And in the same week, Umbrella quietly bought Lumen's
life-sciences cloud division — the

4780
06:33:02,190 --> 06:33:08,545
servers that ran half the world's drug discovery —
for a price Crane's successor accepted

4781
06:33:08,565 --> 06:33:12,270
without negotiating, because Lumen
needed the money.

4782
06:33:15,091 --> 06:33:18,783
Assessment. Adversarial model defeated.

4783
06:33:26,386 --> 06:33:32,202
Sebastian Crane came to Wilmington in April. Not to
the tower. To the old building on

4784
06:33:32,222 --> 06:33:38,520
the south bank, with its faded bricks and its small
red-and-white sign, where he had somehow

4785
06:33:38,540 --> 06:33:44,562
learned — Oracle, perhaps, one last time — that the
Director still kept an office on the

4786
06:33:44,582 --> 06:33:45,386
third floor.

4787
06:33:45,957 --> 06:33:50,737
He stood in the parking lot in the rain until
Hale came out and brought him in.

4788
06:33:51,426 --> 06:33:55,627
It was a trap. The whole six months. You taught it
to predict you, so you could make it

4789
06:33:55,647 --> 06:33:55,918
wrong.

4790
06:33:56,439 --> 06:33:57,078
Yes.

4791
06:33:57,598 --> 06:34:02,062
I spent nine billion dollars trying to build what you
have. And you beat me with the one

4792
06:34:02,082 --> 06:34:05,118
thing I never thought to model. You
beat me by being boring.

4793
06:34:05,640 --> 06:34:11,721
Everyone underestimates boring, Sebastian. It's the
most dangerous thing a man can be.

4794
06:34:13,741 --> 06:34:14,830
What are you?

4795
06:34:16,849 --> 06:34:22,932
The Director did not answer. He looked at the young
man dripping on his carpet — brilliant,

4796
06:34:22,952 --> 06:34:28,766
arrogant, ruined, and still, even now, even here,
burning with that childish total envy

4797
06:34:28,786 --> 06:34:34,465
— and the Cold-Read showed him something that he
had seen before, in a break room, in

4798
06:34:34,485 --> 06:34:36,947
the face of a young woman at a piano.

4799
06:34:37,517 --> 06:34:42,102
Crane did not want revenge. He wanted to be
near it. Whatever it was.

4800
06:34:42,791 --> 06:34:44,392
He wants to serve.

4801
06:34:44,914 --> 06:34:48,818
He wants to understand. For a man like
him, it's the same thing.

4802
06:34:50,718 --> 06:34:55,725
I can't tell you what I am. But I can offer you a
job. Umbrella has just acquired half

4803
06:34:55,745 --> 06:35:00,751
the drug-discovery servers on Earth, and it has no
one who understands them as well as

4804
06:35:00,771 --> 06:35:05,719
you do. You'd report to me. You'd have no board.
And you would be close enough, every

4805
06:35:05,739 --> 06:35:08,875
day, to keep trying to model me —
and to keep failing.

4806
06:35:11,395 --> 06:35:17,255
Sebastian Crane stood in the corner office with the
water stain shaped like Florida, and

4807
06:35:17,275 --> 06:35:23,270
laughed. It was a strange laugh — half despair, half
delight — and it went on for a while.

4808
06:35:23,959 --> 06:35:25,219
When do I start?

4809
06:35:27,240 --> 06:35:33,121
It was the third time someone had offered themselves
to him in exactly that way. The Adolf

4810
06:35:33,141 --> 06:35:35,417
half of the mind was keeping count.

4811
06:35:35,987 --> 06:35:42,124
Evelyn Marsh, at a piano: can I help? Sir Martin
Ellery, in front of the glass: when do

4812
06:35:42,144 --> 06:35:48,424
I start? And now a boy who had built a machine to see
God, and seen it, and wanted a desk

4813
06:35:48,444 --> 06:35:49,132
beside it.

4814
06:36:09,190 --> 06:36:14,809
There was one company on Earth that Albert Wesker
could not buy, and it was the only one

4815
06:36:14,829 --> 06:36:16,987
that frightened Catherine Adeyemi.

4816
06:36:17,558 --> 06:36:23,823
Tianhe Genomics was headquartered in Shenzhen, in a
tower of green glass that looked across

4817
06:36:23,843 --> 06:36:29,833
the water at Hong Kong. It had begun, twenty years
before, as a gene-sequencing company

4818
06:36:29,853 --> 06:36:36,188
that sold its services cheaply to hospitals across
China, and it had grown, with the patient

4819
06:36:36,208 --> 06:36:42,681
support of the Chinese state, into something that the
American intelligence services described

4820
06:36:42,701 --> 06:36:49,174
in their classified reports as a national champion, and
that Tianhe's own employees described,

4821
06:36:49,194 --> 06:36:51,799
on their first morning, as the future.

4822
06:36:52,369 --> 06:36:58,774
It had sequenced the genomes of four hundred million
people. It had the largest biological

4823
06:36:58,794 --> 06:37:04,983
database in human history. And it answered, in the
end, not to its shareholders, but to

4824
06:37:05,003 --> 06:37:05,555
Beijing.

4825
06:37:07,624 --> 06:37:12,020
It had also, since twenty thirty-one, been
trying to steal from Umbrella.

4826
06:37:12,591 --> 06:37:18,377
Not with rifles. With patience. Umbrella's Singapore
plant employed four thousand people.

4827
06:37:18,397 --> 06:37:23,923
Nine of them, Hale's people had discovered over
three years, were reporting — through

4828
06:37:23,943 --> 06:37:29,730
relatives, through friends, through a very old and
very careful network — to the Ministry

4829
06:37:29,750 --> 06:37:35,342
of State Security in Beijing. Hale had not removed
them. The Director had told him not

4830
06:37:35,362 --> 06:37:41,410
to. It was the same principle as Howard Lessing: a spy
you have found is a radio you control.

4831
06:37:41,979 --> 06:37:48,189
But in the spring of twenty thirty-four, the radio
began to receive something the Director

4832
06:37:48,209 --> 06:37:54,350
had not put there. Somebody in Beijing had begun
asking the nine to find out one specific

4833
06:37:54,370 --> 06:37:58,573
thing. Not the formula for Aeterna. Not
